\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA LOCAL BONDS-Yields rise as  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  sag\b0\par\par\uc2 537 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Mai 2010\par 08:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Indonesian, Philippine bond yields inch higher\par \par \uc2 * Thailand bond yields turn lower\par \par \uc2 By Saikat Chatterjee\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, May 25 (Reuters) - Asian local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bond yields rose on Tuesday as weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  took some sheen off Asia's high-performing debt.\par \par \uc2 A strong rebound in growth in the continent's economies, rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and low debt-to-GDP ratios have fuelled demand among global funds for Asian government debt in recent months.\par \par \uc2 But the euro zone's debt problems have triggered a wave of risk aversion in world markets leading to a marked slowdown in new capital inflows, which in turn has sapped the rally in emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The ringgit <MYR=>, which is the star performer in Asia, up by more than 8 percent until the end of April, has seen its year-to-date gains cut sharply to a little above 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 Central banks in \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Philippines were seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , both of which have fallen to their lowest in several months. [ID:nSGE64O03Q]\par \par \uc2 By early afternoon, the ten-year Indonesian bond yield <ID10YT=RR> was up six basis points at 9.03 percent, while the four-year bond yield in the Philippines <PHBD0567=> was two basis points higher at 5.94 percent, traders said.\par \par \uc2 "Risk aversion is still there and that has reduced the flows into local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds," said Lum Choong Kuan, head of fixed income at CIMB Investment Bank. "Although there has been some selling in Thailand, we are still seeing a lot of demand for rupiah and ringgit assets."\par \par \uc2 Thomson Reuters data shows Indonesian local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds in dollar terms have lost more than 2 percent in the past month cutting their year-to-date returns to 10 percent.\par \par \uc2 The latest EPFR data shows foreign fund inflows into emerging market bond funds have slowed to a trickle in recent weeks, a far cry from more than $1 billion per week seen in March.\par \par \uc2 In Thailand, however, yields fell as investors expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep rates on hold next week because of the negative impact of months of political unrest on its economy.\par \par \uc2 DBS expects the economy to contract 9 percent on a seasonally adjusted annualised rate in the second quarter after expanding at 15 percent in the March quarter because of the riots.\par \par \uc2 The five-year bond yield <TH5YT=RR> eased by six basis points to 3.11 percent, just shy of its lowest level since end April.\par \par \uc2 A two-month political standoff ended last week but economists said it could take months for the economy to get back to rates of growth seen in the March quarter. This puts any chances of a near-term rate hike in the freezer for now.\par \par \uc2 "While we believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still keen to raise interest rates to a more neutral level, this will require a more stable political backdrop ... the situation remains fluid, but the possibility of fresh elections in the fourth quarter may allow them to begin tightening," Standard Chartered Bank said. (Editing by Chris Lewis & Jan Dahinten)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-BONDS/|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|IF|SI|Z|PHD|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100525e65p0009i\par }\page 