\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Indonesia Shares Likely Up;2650 Resistance Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2010\par 04:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0209 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares may rebound, with gains in several Asian markets such as in Singapore, Taiwan and China likely providing support, traders say; tip resistance at 2650 after ending down 2.6% Friday at 2623.221. "Overall, I think the market will consolidate after the recent selloff, but has potential to rebound on hopes of higher dividend payments," says trader at First Asia Capital. Car maker Astra (ASII.JK) may gain after company says it may propose to give IDR870 per share dividend at shareholders' meeting in June, while bellwether Telkom (TLKM.JK) may gain on bargain buying. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds steady as investors focus on Agricultural Bank of China's IPO plan after Economic Observer reports Friday China's securities regulator may review bank's IPO proposal on May 28; Agricultural Bank eyes dual listing in Shanghai, HK that could raise US$20 billion-US$30 billion, people familiar with situation say earlier. "If the report is true, the bank could launch its A-share IPO as early as next month, adding liquidity pressure to the money market," says Beijing-based trader with local insurer. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 125.33, nearly unchanged vs 125.32 last close, likely in narrow 125.25-125.35 band rest of session. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares likely to rebound on bargain-buying after key index fell 12.0% over past 3 weeks. Still, benchmark index unlikely to rise sharply; "some players are hoping that prices will recover, but I think other investors will sell amid rising concerns that Vietnam is suffering a huge trade deficit this year, hurting the macroeconomic conditions," says Hanoi-based trader. Tips key index in 485-488 range after ending down 3.9% at 483.69 Friday. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0200 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Pakistan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to keep key interest rate steady for third straight policy meeting later today as it tries to tether high prices while supporting nascent economic recovery; all 11 economists in Dow Jones poll expect \uc2 State Bank of Pakistan\uc2  to keep discount rate steady at 12.50%. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reviews monetary policy every two months, last met March 27. "We do not expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce its policy rate...owing to persistent inflation and risks to fiscal account," says Raza Khan Niazi, research analyst at Invisor Securities. "At the same time, we do not expect it to hamper the economic recovery by raising the interest rate," he adds. Inflation accelerated to 13.26% in April from 12.90% in March while economy projected to grow 2.5%-3.5% this fiscal year ending June, faster than last year's 2% expansion. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] Chances of ECB stepping in to stop EUR's fall still slim for now as EUR/USD remains above long-term fair value of 1.20, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. Notes, volatility much higher in 2008 after Lehman's collapse, but ECB still refrained from entering \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets then. Adds EUR's history has only one major bout of official EUR/USD action, in September 2000, where pair fell to 0.86 vs 1.17 at January 1999 start of trade. But says risk of European crisis pushing EUR much lower vs fair value levels in disorderly manner means ECB may need several episodes of either unilateral or co-ordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2010-2020. Advises investors to watch for cues such as European policymakers' rhetoric that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets aren't reflecting fundamentals, and rhetoric that trading has become disorderly. EUR/USD last at 1.2500 vs 1.2583 late NY trade Friday. (wei-zhe.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds mixed; 5-year note lower as banks take profit amid strong local shares (Taiex last +0.7%), but solid demand from securities houses boosts 10-year issue, says local brokerage trader. 5-year yield last at 0.9655% vs 0.9639% Friday's close, 10-year yield at 1.3790% vs 1.3830%. Other dated bonds untraded as players stay on sidelines ahead of start of when-issued trade for reopened 10-year bond tomorrow. Tips yield for when-issued trade (for 10-year bond) to fall below 1.4000% as lingering concerns over Europe's debt crisis may fuel demand for safe-haven government securities. Tips 5-year yield in tight 0.9500%-0.9700% band for rest of session, 10-year yield in 1.3500%-1.4000%. (crystal.hsu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0155 GMT [Dow Jones] Market participants cautious after South Korean President Lee Myung-bak's public address, pledging stern measures to hold North Korea accountable for sinking of South Korean patrol ship, refer case to UN Security Council, suspend trade, exchanges with North, etc. "The tone of President Lee's comment sounded stronger than expected," says \uc2 Samsung Securities\uc2 ' strategist Oh Hyun-Seok; "But market participants don't seem to be taking his comments as a new negative that could heighten geopolitical risk and discount our stocks further." Mirae Asset Securities' market analyst Lee Jae-hoon says "geopolitical risk related to the sinking of the South Korean naval ship seem to be largely priced in current stock prices"; adds "People have learned from previous cases that they made profits when buying on dips caused by geopolitical risk. Yes, investors are still taking a cautious stance, but they know the market may overcome this risk unless the two Koreas engage in military skirmishes due to this issue." Kospi down 0.4% at 1593.75. (soo-kyung.seo@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0154 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares open up led by property developers on hopes of short-term halt in domestic property tightening. Shanghai Composite Index up 1.4% at 2621.70, faces immediate resistance at 2650. "The technical rebound which extended from Friday is also fuelled by growing expectations that the authorities would not implement more aggressive property cooling measures as previously anticipated so as not to overly impact broader economic growth," says Huatai Securities analyst Zhou Lin. Among actives, \uc2 China Vanke Co\uc2 . (000002.SZ) up 3.5% at CNY7.66, \uc2 Poly Real Estate Group\uc2  (600048.SH) up 4.1% at CNY11.62. Shenzhen Composite Index up 2.2% at 1030.18. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0152 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR slightly higher at 9,290 in thin volume vs 9,280 late Friday as players still cautious over euro-zone sovereign debt problems, dealers say; add so far trading mostly among local players as foreigners remain on sidelines. "The dollar remains well bid against most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this morning," dealer says; 9,275-9,325 range tipped. Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if pair rises above 9,300. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] May HSI futures down 0.4% at 19,360, suggests any further falls in cash market likely limited after setting fresh year-to-date low of 19,276 Thursday. Still contract ended at 111-point discount to cash Thursday, suggests near-term direction remains negative. Open interest down 279 lots at 77,495. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0148 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW still trading sharply higher at 1,213.4 vs 1,194.1 late Thursday in Seoul amid lingering geopolitical concerns between two Koreas, but little moved from earlier 1,214.3 level after South Korean President says South Korea to refer North Korea case to U.N. Security Council, as such measure had been largely anticipated. But traders note, sentiment may remain fragile for couple more days as players likely to keep alert on how other countries including North Korea, U.S. respond. "There doesn't seem to be anything special (or surprising) in the speech. It's in the principles, but his tone of speech is strong," says local bank trader. Adding, geopolitical concerns may keep USD/KRW unstable for a while. Another trader tips pair in wide 1,190-1,230 range, with upside capped by chance of USD-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0143 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares stage broad technical rebound, +0.7% at 7288.54 after closing at 3.5-month low of 7237.71 Friday; though thin volume suggests buying interest not strong, says Masterlink Investment Advisory vice president Tom Tang. "There aren't many buyers in the market as stocks fell a lot and are still below the cost of many investors," he says; tips cap at 7300-7350 today. "If (trading) volume doesn't pick up, Taiwan shares will continue consolidating in the mid-term," he says. Tech shares rebound on positive cue from Wall Street: TSMC (2330.TW) +0.3% to NT$59.0, Hon Hai Precision (2317.TW) +1.2% to NT$132.50, MediaTek (2454.TW) +1.2% to NT$499.0. Airlines also going strong after Taiwan, China agree expanding cross-strait passenger flights by 37% from June and adding Hongqiao Airport in Shanghai to quota: China Airlines (2610.TW) +3.5% to NT$13.30, \uc2 EVA Air\uc2  (2618.TW) +2.7% to NT$16.85. (alex.pevzner@dowjones.com) [ 05-23-10 2209ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100524e65o0001q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\png