\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Slightly Up; 9,275-9,325 Range Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,550 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2010\par 03:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0152 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR slightly higher at 9,290 in thin volume vs 9,280 late Friday as players still cautious over euro-zone sovereign debt problems, dealers say; add so far trading mostly among local players as foreigners remain on sidelines. "The dollar remains well bid against most regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this morning," dealer says; 9,275-9,325 range tipped. Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  expected to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if pair rises above 9,300. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] May HSI futures down 0.4% at 19,360, suggests any further falls in cash market likely limited after setting fresh year-to-date low of 19,276 Thursday. Still contract ended at 111-point discount to cash Thursday, suggests near-term direction remains negative. Open interest down 279 lots at 77,495. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0148 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW still trading sharply higher at 1,213.4 vs 1,194.1 late Thursday in Seoul amid lingering geopolitical concerns between two Koreas, but little moved from earlier 1,214.3 level after South Korean President says South Korea to refer North Korea case to U.N. Security Council, as such measure had been largely anticipated. But traders note, sentiment may remain fragile for couple more days as players likely to keep alert on how other countries including North Korea, U.S. respond. "There doesn't seem to be anything special (or surprising) in the speech. It's in the principles, but his tone of speech is strong," says local bank trader. Adding, geopolitical concerns may keep USD/KRW unstable for a while. Another trader tips pair in wide 1,190-1,230 range, with upside capped by chance of USD-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0143 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares stage broad technical rebound, +0.7% at 7288.54 after closing at 3.5-month low of 7237.71 Friday; though thin volume suggests buying interest not strong, says Masterlink Investment Advisory vice president Tom Tang. "There aren't many buyers in the market as stocks fell a lot and are still below the cost of many investors," he says; tips cap at 7300-7350 today. "If (trading) volume doesn't pick up, Taiwan shares will continue consolidating in the mid-term," he says. Tech shares rebound on positive cue from Wall Street: TSMC (2330.TW) +0.3% to NT$59.0, Hon Hai Precision (2317.TW) +1.2% to NT$132.50, MediaTek (2454.TW) +1.2% to NT$499.0. Airlines also going strong after Taiwan, China agree expanding cross-strait passenger flights by 37% from June and adding Hongqiao Airport in Shanghai to quota: China Airlines (2610.TW) +3.5% to NT$13.30, \uc2 EVA Air\uc2  (2618.TW) +2.7% to NT$16.85. (alex.pevzner@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0142 GMT [Dow Jones] South Korean President Lee saying will refer alleged North Korean sinking of South Korean warship to UN Security Council is prompting wide range of investors in Asia to further cut exposure to risk-sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with safe-haven JPY benefiting most, says Satoshi Okagawa, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at \uc2 Sumitomo Mitsui Banking\uc2 . "The main focus this morning is now on the Korean situation, as well as the sell-off in stocks despite Friday's recovery in U.S. share markets." Mounting risk aversion hurting EUR; says EUR/JPY could fall to 111.00 from last 112.38, EUR/USD to 1.2400 from 1.2492. Falls also dragging down USD/JPY; says pair may fall to 89.50 vs last 89.93. Adds \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  CEO saying proposed mining tax in Australia sovereign risk issue also encouraging more players to further trim AUD/JPY, AUD/USD longs. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT [Dow Jones] Copper futures on Shanghai Futures exchange rise modestly, in response to gains on London Metal Exchange Friday amid expectations pace of Beijing's tightening measures may slow due to ongoing sovereign debt crisis in Europe, says Shanghai Cifco Futures analyst Wang Zhouyi; adds metal may rise further over course of day on improved market sentiment. Says copper may rebound this week, but "market sentiment remains fragile as investors are still unsure about economic outlook in Europe as well as in China." Tips nearby resistance at CNY56,000/ton on chart-based cues. Benchmark August copper last +1.8% at CNY54,350/ton, off opening high of CNY54,700/ton. (yue.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0135 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD lower at 32.101 vs 5-month high 32.250 late Friday, as exporters sell to meet month-end settlement needs, investors recover some confidence on risky assets including stocks (Taiex last +0.6%), says foreign bank trader; also on mildly widening NDFs after EUR moves stabilized. "Still, it is too early to conclude whether the Europe's debt crisis is over, or if the rebound in local shares will last." Expects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  late in session as it has been suspected of buying USD these days to lift \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  costs to discourage foreign investors from fleeing local stock market. Tips pair in 32.100-32.200 band rest of session if \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  absent. (crystal.hsu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may fall to catch up on declines on Wall Street on Thursday after local market shut Friday due to public holiday, says Francis Lun at Fulbright; tips index to fall by around 200 points today, to be led by \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) given its exposure to Europe, with its ADRs last down 1.7%. Tips bargain-hunting interest in H-shares likely to limit further falls, after index hit fresh year-to-date low of 19,276 Thursday. Expects psychological 19,000 as near-term support. HSI ends down 0.2% at 19,545.83 Thursday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares getting boost from Wall Street's Friday recovery, but gains modest as ongoing concerns over Europe's debt problems continue to temper investors' appetite. STI +0.3% at 2709.41; resistance at higher end of 2718-2735 technical gap formed late last week. "The STI is beginning to look very attractive," says Roger Tan, head of SIAS Research, noting current 13X P/E; "there is a good chance that the STI will see some strength in June as funds window dress their books for the mid-year close." Market breadth at 2 gainers for every decliner, with most FTSE ST sub-indexes higher. Best performing sectors include shipping, China plays, with FTSE ST Maritime Index +0.9%, FTSE ST China Index +0.7%. Among STI components, notable gainers include \uc2 Hongkong Land\uc2  (H78.SG), +1.3% at US$4.74, SMRT (S53.SG), +1.0% at S$2.10. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tipped to open a tad higher, likely getting fillip from strong close in Wall Street Friday; benchmark PSEi may rise toward initial resistance at 3200 after declining 1.1% Friday to end at 3179.36, with 3150 as immediate support; "While the Dow rose Friday, we're seeing a mixed open in Asia and weaker futures. So we'll likely move in a narrow range today, with a slight upward bias," says Jose Vistan, research head at AB Capital Securities. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures higher amid Kospi's 0.2% loss; 3-year yield down 4 bps at 3.70%, 5-year yield down 5 bps at 4.40%, June futures +20 ticks at 111.34. But traders note KRW's sharp fall to 8-month lows, remain a risk factor on concerns it may trigger sell-off in local bonds by foreigners; "First of all, foreigners aren't net buyers in futures (yet) today. They are either selling a bit or seem sidelined...Foreign investors' moves in futures as well as cash bonds will likely be the most important cue today," says local securities firm trader. But notes, buying interest fairly well supported so far; "investors seem to be starting to shift their focus (back) to the possibility of a slowdown in the economic recovery," in wake of recent euro-zone debt crises amid bearish stock markets around globe of late. Tips 3-year yield in 3.69%-3.79% range for day. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD remains quite fragile and vulnerable to selling from momentum and model investors, says Bank of New Zealand Strategist Mike Jones. Notes \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  comments on Australian resources tax proposals were just another excuse to sell; says a test of 0.8150 later is likely, which clears way for a slump to 0.8070. Last 0.8203, opened session at 0.8359. Adds, to reverse slump, needs some hawkish comments from RBA or upside data surprise in growth figures. "The AUD will probably trade fairly heavily this week." (enda.curran@dowjones.com) [ 05-23-10 2152ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100524e65o0001i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
