\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Off Highs; 9,270-9,330 Range Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2010\par 09:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0729 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  vows to continue \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, senior deputy governor Nasution says won't let IDR fall too sharply. Adds, IDR weakness likely to be temporary. Comments come after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suspected to have sold around $150 million after USD/IDR hit IDR9,340, pair's highest level since March 1, earlier today. "I don't think Bank Indonesia is trying to set a certain (resistance) level for the dollar-rupiah. Its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely only to slow the pair's rise," dealer with foreign bank says. Pair last at 9,280 vs 9,270 late yesterday; 9,270-9,330 range tipped for afternoon session. (im-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0729 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds end up after \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's CNY28 billion, 50-year bond auction receives strong demand from insurance firms; MOF sells 50-year bond at 4.03%, below 4.13%-4.20% range tipped by some analysts. "It's the second issuance of such super-long debt (since the maiden issue in November) in China, so long-term investors are actively bidding for it," says Beijing-based trader with local fund. Shanghai exchange government bond index ends at 125.32 vs 125.25 last close, likely to keep upward bias next week as recent turmoil in global equity markets boosts interests in fixed-income sector. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0724 GMT [Dow Jones] Don't be surprised to see the AUD down at $0.77, says RBS. The bank says that although the current global crisis probably isn't a repeat of 2008/2009 when the AUD fell back into a $0.60-0.73 range, the Aussie will still be treated "as a whipping boy on any signs of banking sector funding stress." The bank adds that it expects the policy measures in Europe to eventually reduce the stress but that it will "take some time for the AUD to regain its feet." The AUD is now at $0.8279. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0721 GMT [Dow Jones] Global equity markets took a sharp hit Thursday weighed by below forecast U.S. data and on fears of tougher regulations out of the euro zone. However, instead of the euro sinking on risk aversion trades it rallied hard with fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  still spooking the markets. EUR/USD breached 1.25 in U.S. trade which reportedly triggered large option-related stops and went on to peak at 1.2673 in Asia Friday. Moves in EUR/CHF were even more dramatic with the SNB probably rejoicing as the cross surged to within a whisker of 1.46 from the day's 1.4140 lows. USD/JPY and EUR/JPY collapsed, the latter printing a fresh 8-year low under 110.00 although both pairs gained ground in Asia after Japan's Finance Minister Kan said excessive JPY strength was not desirable. For Friday, EU Finance ministers meet on EU reforms, European stocks open small up and the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is likely to take a breather. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)-o-\par \par \uc2 0715 GMT [Dow Jones] The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  should start giving up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  battle, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Focus by Nicholas Hastings. The cost, in terms of increased money supply, will soon become more onerous. And the need for a weaker CHF will soon become less imperative. All the same, the SNB will no doubt be reluctant to retire from the battlefield while the risks of another sharp collapse in the EUR remain so high. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0714 GMT [Dow Jones] German lawmakers will vote on the EUR750 billion support plan for fiscally strained euro-zone countries later Friday, but \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  says any related respite is likely to be short lived. "Something is broken when it comes to confidence, and there is little alternative in the short term than to go with the momentum and favor flight-to-quality trades - there is no obvious catalyst for a reversal in the direction of markets," \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  says. BNP notes while yield spreads on peripheral euro-zone government bonds vs German bunds have become less volatile since the ECB began buying bonds in the secondary market, the market action on Thursday points to renewed tension in the short run. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0709 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY off highs as some Japan exporters sell ahead of weekend, says senior dealer at major Tokyo bank. EUR/JPY last at 113.33 vs 114.40 intraday high, 111.75 late NY Thursday. "Some exporters, who had missed an occasion to sell the euro when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunged recently, are now selling." Thinks EUR's bearish momentum remains intact due to euro-zone debt jitters. Investors likely to keep close eye on global stock markets, as weak equities expected raise demand for safe-haven JPY. Tips EUR/JPY's support at 110.00. EUR/USD last at 1.2551 vs 1.2673 intraday high, support eyed at 1.2300. (miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0656 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 1.4% at 1285.58, dealer says poor sentiment in line with weak regional bourses, absence of any positive local leads keeping cautious retail players from picking up battered-down stocks. Techs leading losses (down 2.8%) on concerns local chip makers could be first to feel effects of any economic slowdown caused by euro-zone debt problems. "There uncertainties are all around and the trend is bearish," dealer says; tips immediate support at 1280, then 1250. Market breadth negative with decliners beating advancers 10-to-1; decliners include Proton (5304.KU) down 4.9% at MYR4.51, \uc2 Hong Leong Bank\uc2  (5819.KU) down 2.3% at MYR8.45, JCY (5161.KU) down 4.3% at MYR1.56, AirAsia (5099.KU) down 4.2% at MYR1.15. Advancers are \uc2 Petronas Dagangan\uc2  (5681.KU) +0.2% at MYR8.96, Esso (3042.KU) +0.8% at MYR2.64. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0654 GMT [Dow Jones] June bunds have drifted lower after the 10-year bund futures contract hit a record high Thursday, and WestLB says momentum indicators moved in to overbought territory on a daily basis. However, the near-term upside momentum remains intact, the bank says. Support is seen at 128.11, 127.70 and 127.31. Resistance is pegged at 128.58, 129.00, and 129.73. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0650 GMT [Dow Jones] China April refined copper imports down 2.7% on-year, down 8.1% on-month, at 309,772 tons, partly due to closure of attractive arbitrage window between Shanghai, London, says Shanghai-based trader; adds, Beijing's tightening monetary measures since early this year has dented investment funds as "less liquidity means those speculators can't use copper deals to finance." Says imports still remain at high levels; "the on-year decline was simply because of record-high numbers last year." Copper imports may decline in coming months "as summer is usually a weak consumption season for copper, but recent opening of arbitrage window could prompt some importing activities at trading houses." In January-April period, China imports 1.1 million tons of refined copper, down 0.2% on-year, Customs says. (yue.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0644 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks not expected to turn around although likely to end above morning lows. STI off 1.7% at 2707.35 vs 2693.86 midday, intraday low of 2678.22, supported by gains in Wilmar (F34.SG), U.S. stock futures. "We expect some bottom scooping to take place this afternoon to support prices. However, as we expect further downside in the coming days, we do not advise investors to hold positions over the weekend," says CIMB. Market breadth at 9 decliners for each gainer vs 13 to 1 at midday. Wilmar (F34.SG) +3.8% at S$5.71, bucking decline in all other STI components, as buyers return after steep losses in recent sessions on tax-fraud allegations, which plantation group has denied. Overall market volume light at under 1 billion shares, with good mix of large-caps, small-caps among most active. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0642 GMT [Dow Jones] India interbank call rate, collateralized lending and borrowing obligation, or CBLO, dull on very little borrowing demand on account of reporting Friday, says dealer with state-run bank. Banks need to report cash reserve levels to RBI at end of every fortnight. Overnight call rate on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's negotiated dealing system unchanged vs 3.80% last close, CBLO rate at 2.24% vs 2.00% at open. "There is a lot of cash floating in the system. Banks have their cash requirements covered because the reporting fortnight ends today," dealer says. Total traded amount in call money market at INR42.15 billion vs INR80 billion-INR100 billion normally, traded volumes under CBLO window at INR152.40 billion vs INR500 billion. Dealer notes, rates under call money market, CBLO window likely to shoot up next week, once 3G auction fee payments start pressuring liquidity. Telecom operators have to pay around INR677.19 billion to government within next 10 days as part of spectrum bidding fees. Tips call rate in 3.00%-3.80% range for session. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com) [ 05-21-10 0329ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100521e65l0004u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\