\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Up Further; 9,290-9,340 Range Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,393 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2010\par 03:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0128 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR continues rising to 9,310 from 9,270 late yesterday on festering concerns over euro-zone sovereign debt problems; Bank Indonesia suspected to have sold an order of $50 million at 9,310 to slow pair's rise, according to 3 dealers. "We expect more Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  today," dealer says. 9,290-9,340 range tipped. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0124 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks bruised as investors reluctant to own shares amid heightened concerns over euro-zone problems. CBOE Volatility Index, widely regarded as gauge of market fear, last +29.6%. STI down 2.5% at 2684.07, below 2700 for first time since February. If current year-to-date low of 2665 set in February gives way, next support at 2605 (October 29 low). Market breadth at 29 decliners for every gainer, with all FTSE ST sub-indexes down. "Sell in May and go away is pretty sound advice now. The issue in Europe is not going to go away any time soon. Activity is also going to slow down with the World Cup approaching," says analyst at Singapore bank. Worst hit among STI components include Noble Group (N21.SG) off 7.1% at S$1.58, NOL (N03.SG) off 4.7% at S$1.81, SembMarine (S51.SG) off 4.2% at S$3.66, \uc2 Keppel Corp\uc2 . (BN4.SG) off 4.1% at S$8.44. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares likely to retreat further amid overnight decline of major overseas equities markets, regional market declines on continuing concerns on Europe's fiscal situation. Bellwether PSEi may head toward 3100 support after posting 0.3% decline Thursday to end at 3213.80. "The market will be driven by the fears of investors rather than by fundamentals," says Ron Rodrigo, research head with DBP-Daiwa Securities; but adds, market's 3.5% decline so far this week presents opportunities for investors looking for value creation over the medium term. Tips investors may unload shares in sectors with high exposure to European outlook, such as property sector, which gets support from overseas Filipino workers' remittances. Major property firms include Ayala Land (ALI.PH), Megaworld (MEG.PH). Next resistance 3250. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0117 GMT [Dow Jones] BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) CEO Marius Kloppers says the Australian government's planned Resource Super Profits Tax reduces the value of BHP's iron ore JV with \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) but both partners remain committed to the deal. "(\uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  CEO) Tom (Albanese) and I have spoken about this, and come to the conclusion that this is a deal we should be doing," he tells The Australian newspaper. Some analysts have argued BHP should be raising the US$5.8 billion equalisation payment to Rio to reflect higher iron ore prices; Kloppers says BHP could argue the uncertainty associated with the tax points to the opposite. European Commission approval of the deal remains a key hurdle; Kloppers says there is no indication of timing for a decision but he hopes the miners are coming to the end of a long period of submissions. While doubts still hang over the JV, some suggest the miners are more determined than ever to achieve the synergies as they may now need to offset a higher tax burden. (alex.wilson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0106 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 3.1% at 4181.9, hits fresh 10-month low of 4175.7 after U.S. Financial Reform Bill passed the U.S. Senate by 59/39 votes. S&P 500 futures down about 0.4% on this news, even though it's not unexpected. Japan's Nikkei 225 is down 2.5% following 3.9% overnight drop in S&P 500. Having broken minor technical supports at 4260 and 4212, S&P/ASX 200 has no decent chart support until 4072 (50% retracement of 2000-2010 rise). Index has fallen further than most traders expected today. With volume surging to A$2.3 billion in the first hour of trading, margin calls are obviously having a major impact. It wouldn't take much to trigger an exhaustive dive below 4100 from here. Those still with some cash on the sidelines will be at least considering averaging into major blue chips from current levels. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0102 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds likely to rise, tracking overnight moves in U.S. Treasurys as euro zone problems continue to spook markets, driving money to safer assets. "We expect a lot of buying in Singapore bonds today because the European debt crisis is still at the top of everyone's mind," says trader with international bank; expects yields to fall 5 bps. Yield on 2-year bond +1 bp yesterday to 0.42%; 10-year +4 bps to 2.54%. (gaurav.raghuanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0101 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may fall, tracking drop in U.S. stocks, but downside likely limited after benchmark Shanghai Composite Index ends at lowest level in more than 1 year yesterday; Shanghai index support at key 2500 level after ending down 1.2% at 2555.94. "Investors are concerned that the complicated situations (in domestic and overseas markets) could trigger unexpected policies from the government," says Li Xiaoxuan at Shenyin Wanguo Securities. Xinjiang-related stocks may rise after support package for region unveiled. Shenzhen index ends down 1.4% at 983.87. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0101 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD bounces, with move driven by Japanese bargain-hunters as RBA appears for now to be on sidelines, market observers say; pair lifted from around 0.8100 to 0.8241 in space of an hour, last at 0.8182. As long as market liquidity remains comparatively robust, RBA is less likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even as market is very jittery and rumor-driven; that was the pattern of events in 2008 when it fell by 40% in the space of weeks as confidence in global markets evaporated. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0055 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped to rise further on broad USD strength amid mounting concerns on euro zone's fiscal situation; pair tipped to move towards 46.20, then 46.50 after climbing to 12-week high 45.99 late Thursday. "We expect a continuation of sell-off on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says \uc2 Marcelo Ayes\uc2 , senior vice president at \uc2 Rizal Commercial Banking Corp\uc2 ; next support 45.80. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0051 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely to open lower vs 32.175 late yesterday (3.5-month high) on some exporter selling, after pair rose yesterday on suspected last-minute \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying, says local bank trader. Adds pair may reverse decline later on expected weak local shares, despite island's strong 1Q GDP data. "Uncertainties over Europe's debt problems will dominate the market today." Doesn't expect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  if pair in tandem with regional trend, especially USD/KRW. Tips USD/TWD in 32.000-32.200 band for day. (crystal.hsu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0045 GMT [Dow Jones] Bargain-buying lifting AUD early after unit witnessed sharpest decline since darkest days of credit crisis, says BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Mike Jones. "The Aussie fundamentals are still fairly solid," says selling of unit was "panic-induced." AUD/USD last 0.8206 from 0.8077 early. Says AUD/EUR can recoup losses easier than AUD/USD, but expects volatility to continue with much depending on Asian equities. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0043 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY may trade higher, tracking offshore gains in USD, recent comments by Chinese officials to dampen expectations for yuan appreciation. "In the offshore market, NDFs are also trading higher based on the strength of the dollar; perhaps the spot market may follow suit today," said a Beijing-based local bank trader. Trading range tipped to be between 6.8270-6.8285 after yesterday's OTC close at 6.8279. Central parity tipped at 6.8278 vs yesterday's 6.8277. (esther.fung@dowjones.com) [ 05-20-10 2128ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100521e65l0001c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
