\par\par \b \uc2 Bank  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  To Continue  \highlight22\uc2 Intervening \highlight\uc2  If Necessary -Senior Deputy Governor\b0\par\par\uc2 129 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 2010\par 09:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to slow the rupiah's fall, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's senior deputy governor, Darmin Nasution, said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "The rupiah's fall will be temporary. We will remain in the market," Nasution told reporters.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of having sold around $300 million earlier Wednesday around IDR9,160 after the local unit came under selling pressures following Germany's move to forbid naked short selling, which sparked risk aversion.\par \par \uc2 At 0730 GMT, the dollar was trading at IDR9,175 compared with IDR9,110 late Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 -By Farida Husna; Dow Jones Newswires; 62-21 39831277; I-Made.Sentana@dowjones.com [ 19-05-10 0739GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100519e65j000n5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
