\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA: As Asian Reserves Rise, Euro May Fall Into Disfavor\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Roman   \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column   \par \uc2 823 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2010\par 06:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose 1.5% in April from March as continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets offset a drop in the value of central banks' euro-denominated holdings.\par \par \uc2 That trend that may emerge as an additional negative for the euro if it forces some of the world's top reserve holders to review the preference they've shown for the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent years.\par \par \uc2 According to calculations by Dow Jones Newswires, reserves held by 11 key Asian central banks, not including China's, stood at US$2.810 trillion at the end of April, up from US$2.777 trillion at the end of March. That compares with a 0.3% increase posted in March from February.\par \par \uc2 China, which releases its data on a quarterly basis, reported US$2.447 trillion in reserves at the end of March.\par \par \uc2 Combined, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings account for around two-thirds of the world's overall amount, and analysts believe some 20%-25% of that amount is in euros. That's likely below the international average of 27.4% as of last December, according to International Monetary Fund data, but Asia's percentage has grown steadily in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Worries about a possible reduction of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  euro holdings hit the market earlier this year and are gaining pace as the euro loses ground against the dollar, eroding its status as the top alternative to the greenback. That's important because the latest data show that while market speculators (including hedge funds) are active euro sellers, observers believe most "real-money" managers like central banks and pension funds haven't yet joined the euro-bashing party.\par \par \uc2 In a recent report, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  analysts said the euro-zone's structural problems and handling of the Greek debt crisis have "severely damaged" the notion--particularly among managers of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve funds--that euros can be held as a "safe alternative" to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 So far this year, the euro has lost 11% against the dollar; that includes a 1.6% drop in April and a 4.9% drop so far in May. But that may be just the beginning if Asian central banks start to unload euros.\par \par \uc2 In 2002, when the euro replaced individual euro-zone \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it accounted for only 23.8% of global reserves. It's up 50% against the dollar since, supported by the continued decrease in the dollar as a percentage of global reserves as central banks have diversified in favor of the euro.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, strong purchases of dollars in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets continued in many Asian countries in April. That matches the recent trend as central banks remain wary of allowing their local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise on the strength of sustained flows into fast-growing Asian markets.\par \par \uc2 Seven Asian central banks reported reserves at all-time highs, including Indonesia's, where reserves grew a whopping 9.5% on-month in April on the back of continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been trying to contain the appreciation of the rupiah, one of Asia's best performers this year with a 3.9% gain against the dollar since January on top of a 12% rise in 2009.\par \par \uc2 Strong reserve increases in Singapore and South Korea also were likely driven by continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 Following are the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves for Asia's 12 leading economies. All numbers are in billions of U.S. dollars.\par \par \uc2 The total number excludes China.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par             April 10     April 09     March 10     As Of \par TOTAL        2,810       2,455         2,777   end April Ex China \par  \par China        2,447       1,954         2,447   end March \par Japan        1.047       1,011         1,043   end April \par India        279.6       251.7         279.1   30-Apr \par Indonesia     78.6        56.7          71.4   end April \par Korea        278.9       212.5         272.3   end April \par Malaysia        96        87.7          95.3   30-Apr \par Pakistan        15        11.1            15   30-Apr \par Philippines     47        39.5          46.2   end April \par Singapore    203.4       170.1         196.4   end April \par Taiwan       357.6       304.7           355   end April \par Thailand     147.6         117         144.2   30-Apr \par Hong Kong    259.2       193.4         258.8   end April \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (David Roman, a regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reporter, has been a Dow Jones correspondent since 2002, covering economic and corporate news. He can be reached at 65 6415 4045 or by e-mail: david.roman@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with