\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Asian Shares Come Off Lows; Exporters Hurt Tokyo\b0\par\par\uc2 1,144 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2010\par 06:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds information, quotes, updates/adds market levels)\par \par \uc2 By Shri Navaratnam and Philip Vahn\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian shares recovered some ground Friday as investors were soothed by the realization that a trading glitch was partly behind Wall Street's slide overnight and began to bargain hunt.\par \par \uc2 However, concerns that Greece's debt problems could engulf the weaker European countries continued to linger.\par \par \uc2 In Tokyo, the market remained under pressure as the recent gains in the yen damped exporter stocks.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Barrett, managing director at Commodity Broking Services in Sydney, referring to the early drop in Asian markets, said investors may have overreacted.\par \par \uc2 "People are factoring in a disaster scenario. If that contagion fear (of the Greece fiscal crisis spreading to other European countries) is real why did European markets only fall 1.0% to 1.5%?" he said.\par \par \uc2 In a note to clients \uc2 UBS\uc2  analysts said: "In our view, the lack of a clear 'end game' for sovereign risk in Europe means that despite recent sharp declines in markets, this is not a buying opportunity. Positions may still be unwound."\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei 225 was down 2.7% while Australia's S&P/ASX 200 was down 0.8% at 4547 after hitting an eight-month of low 4427.30 earlier. South Korea's Kospi Composite fell 2.0%, Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index lost 0.8%, Taiwan's main index was off 0.4% and the Shanghai Composite index was down 0.3%.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were up 65 points in screen trade.\par \par \uc2 The euro, which plumbed 14-month lows of $1.2510 against the U.S. dollar Thursday, recovered some ground in Asian trade although analysts expect more pain ahead for the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Korean won bounced back after hitting a three-month low against the U.S. dollar of KRW1,169.5 earlier. It was trading at KRW1,163.9.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average at one point on Thursday skidded as much as 9.2%, the biggest intraday drop in its history, before closing down 3.2%. The intraday dive appeared to be triggered in part by a breakdown of trading systems.\par \par \uc2 In Tokyo, \uc2 Sony\uc2  was down 5.3% while Panasonic lost 5.1%, \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  was off 4.8% and \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  dropped 2.8%. \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  slipped 5.9% after saying late Thursday April same-store sales at its Uniqlo casual clothing chain fell 12.4% on-year.\par \par \uc2 Nintendo fell 10.0% after the video game giant said Thursday its net profit dropped to Y228.64 billion in the fiscal year ended March 31, from Y279.09 billion a year earlier; it also expects profit and sales to fall in the current year.\par \par \uc2 Metal plays were leading losses in China, hurt by sharp declines in metal prices in Asian trade. Baoshan Iron & Steel lost 4.0% and Yunnan Copper fell 4.0%.\par \par \uc2 Shanghai benchmark copper futures were trading 3.3% lower at CNY54,980 per metric ton. The red metal was also down 2.0% on the London Metals Exchange.\par \par \uc2 Financial stocks led the Australian market lower, with ANZ off 3.1% and \uc2 Westpac\uc2  down 3.5%. In the resources sector, BHP Billiton lost 1.3% and \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  was off 2.0%.\par \par \uc2 Financials were also weaker in Seoul. Shinhan Financial lost 3.7% while tech bellwether \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  gave up 2.7%. Daewoo Shipbuilding slipped 2.5%, but was outperforming its peers after the Financial News reported it had won a $1.6 billion contract to build a floating production storage and offloading vessel.\par \par \uc2 In Hong Kong, Swire Pacific rose 2.4% after its property unit \uc2 Swire Properties\uc2  said Thursday it has shelved its US$3.09 billion Hong Kong initial public offering. Traders said the move was viewed positively, given prevailing weak property market conditions in the city state.\par \par \uc2 "Some investors were also concerned with the dilution effect of Swire Pacific's holding in its property unit's spinoff, which may also hurt investment value of Swire Pacific in the long-term," said First Shanghai's Linus Yip.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, Malaysian shares were 0.6% lower, Singapore's Straits Times Index fell 0.8%, India's Sensex was down 1.1% and New Zealand's NZX-50 was 1.5% lower.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the euro was at $1.2712, compared with $1.2599 late in New York, and was fetching Y117.55 against the yen, from Y113.26, but well below Y120.12 on Wednesday. The dollar was at Y92.51, from Y89.92.\par \par \uc2 Despite the steadier euro, "it only seems a matter of time before the key (1.2500) support level breaks" against the dollar, said Mark Smith, an economist at ANZ bank in Wellington.\par \par \uc2 "On the face of it, the overnight market reaction may have been overdone. But the question increasingly asked is where the mark-to-market losses on European debt will ultimately reside, and this is looking to be mostly in European financial institutions. We have now moved beyond a mere Greek debt crisis," Smith said.\par \par \uc2 Markets are also watching the outcome of Thursday's U.K. elections, with results so far showing the opposition Conservative Party leading the seats-count but could fall short of a majority, leading to a hung parliament. The British pound was at $1.4903, up from $1.4832 late in New York.\par \par \uc2 The sharp falls in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted some central banks in the region to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to slow the greenback's rise. The central banks of \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Philippines \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , local traders and dealers said.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia sold some $150 million to support the rupiah, while the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $50 million to $100 million for pesos, market participants estimated.\par \par \uc2 Japanese government bond futures were sharply higher on stronger U.S. Treasurys. The lead contract was up 0.19 at 140.5 points after briefly tapping 140.10, the highest level since March 10.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was at $1,199.00 per troy ounce, down $9.80 from the New York close.\par \par \uc2 Nymex June crude oil futures were down 41 cents at $76.70 a barrel on Globex, after falling 3.6% Thursday to the lowest close since mid-March.\par \par \uc2 -Shri Navaratnam, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4140; shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 07-05-10 0431GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1106 4125 5037 62560   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100507e657000ee\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
