\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Gives Up Some Gains; 9,200-9,400 Eyed Mon\b0\par\par\uc2 1,569 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2010\par 10:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0846 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR gives up some of its gains as participants square positions ahead of weekend after suspected Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , dealers say; pair now at 9,215, off 9,370 intraday high, but still above 9,190 late yesterday; \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suspected to have sold $150 million today at 9,300 and 9,350; its acting governor Nasution says \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to maintain presence in market to avoid "extreme volatility." "Most dollar sellers in the afternoon were the dollar buyers earlier today," a dealer says; dealers tip wide 9,200-9,400 range for Monday, given lingering uncertainties over euro zone debt problems; (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0839 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian Treasurer Wayne Swan set to unveil Tuesday a significant improvement in government's financial position, fueled by forecasts of stronger growth. 2010-11 budget will likely see deficit of A$34.3 billion, down from expected final deficit in 2009-10 of A$48 billion, according to Dow Jones survey of 16 economists Friday. Economic growth in 2010-11 expected to accelerate to healthy 3.5%, as commodity price gains turbo-charge economy. Inflation expected to remain at benign 2.8% in 2010-11, still within RBA's 2%-3% target. Having already unveiled major tax system overhaul last weekend -- including controversial plan to levy new 40% tax on miners to help pay for reduction in company taxes, increase in amount employers must pay into workers' retirement savings -- Labor government likely to hold back any major policy sweeteners until election, which due within months, draws nearer. Swan will announce budget at 0930 GMT Tuesday. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0837 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds mixed late; local funds' bargain-buying alternated with mild selling by foreign funds, says dealer with local brokerage. "Interest rate swaps fell sharply in the morning in reaction to the steep fall on US stocks but has since turned mixed with the one-year last up 8 basis points," says dealer. Adds, bonds, however, fairly steady. Tips yields to stay in range Monday, ahead of March industrial production data (expected at 1201 GMT Tuesday), policy rate decision (1000 GMT Thursday) and 1Q GDP data (1000 GMT Friday). "With these major data due, most investors are likely to stay on the sidelines," says dealer. Yields on September 2011 MGS down 5 bps at 2.62%, April 2014 MGS +5 bps at 2.59%, August 2015 MGS down 1 bp at 3.59%, September 2017 MGS, November 2019 MGS and April 2030 MGS all flat at 3.80%, 4.08%, 4.50% respectively. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0837 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts slip more as the stark reality of a hung parliament dawns on people, says Marc Ostwald at Monument Securities. Gilts had been supported by safe haven flows in recent sessions, which is surprising given the size of the UK's budget deficit, says Ostwald and with the possibility of another election later this year, gilts will see more selling pressure. With markets focussing on the election, PPI data has gone relatively unnoticed. Output producer prices rose 1.4% on month and 5.7% on year vs expectations of 0.5% and 4.8%, respectively. UK June gilts at 116.50 after slipping to 116.39 session low. The 10-year gilt currently yields 3.884%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0835 GMT [Dow Jones] Euro-zone overnight money market rates Friday appear little changed but mask a sharp rise in risk aversion across the board, participants in London and Frankfurt say. Overnight rates remain around the 0.35% rate, but liquidity is vanishing from longer maturities, due to uncertainty over how the ECB intends to proceed when its EUR442 billion 12-month tender expires in July. "Everything beyond that date is getting harder to price," one trader says. All eyes on an ECB conference call with major banks at 0830 GMT. (GTS) Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 0835 GMT [Dow Jones] If recent trends in government bond markets continue for another week or two, "the ECB will have no choice but to step in and start buying government bonds," says \uc2 Evolution Securities\uc2  analyst Gary Jenkins. Adds that while ECB President Trichet opted to deny that any form of quantitative easing had been discussed at the bank's meeting Thursday, if its recent track record is anything to go by, that doesn't mean it won't soon be buying government debt. "They were equally adamant that the collateral rules would not be changed only a couple of months ago," Jenkins says in reference to a recent change in the rules. (art.patnaude@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0830 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD up at 31.850 vs 31.749 late yesterday, highest since March 26, with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected of buying, say two local bank traders. Pair briefly traded lower in afternoon on exporter selling around 31.750-31.800, short-covering of TWD by traders, says one; but notes \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely prefers USD/TWD to be higher, tracking other USD/Asians. Tips pair in 31.500-31.800 band Monday. Volume US$1.54 billion vs US$1.44 billion yesterday. Warns concerns over Europe's debt problems still weighing on investor confidence; "if shares continue to fall because of Europe, this will cause more foreign funds to flow out of Taiwan and Asia." (charmian.kok@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0827 GMT [Dow Jones] Elevated demand for ECB overnight funds, with marginal lending well above EUR1Bln throughout this week, indicates heightened liquidity concerns across the zone. "An increasing number of banks cannot get sufficient funding in the market," says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 's Juergen Michels. "It's not only the Greek banks, but probably also banks from countries which have seen their sovereign debt ratings under pressure, namely Spain, Portugal and Ireland," he says. "Another explanation could be that banks have underestimated their liquidity needs this week, not bidding enough in the ECB's weekly tender. Demand in next week's refinancing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be significantly higher." (nina.koeppen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0825 GMT [Dow Jones] The zloty remains considerably weakened in thin trade as European sovereign debt concerns continue to weigh, a dealer says. The EUR/PLN, still incredibly sensitive to any EUR/USD move, is seen in a 4.18-4.2250 range Friday. "We're counting on a calming of emotions after three days of escalating fear," the dealer says. EUR/PLN trades at 4.1869 from 4.1135 late Thursday, USD/PLN at 3.2916 from 3.2070. Average yield on 2-year paper is at 4.75% versus 4.69% late Thursday, on 5-year at 5.46% versus 5.38%, on 10-year at 5.88% versus 5.83%. (marynia.kruk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0824 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro gains across the board after the German Finance Minister does his best to talk up support for Greece, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  sounds more positive on Italy and European equity bourses come off their lows. EUR/USD prints the day's high of 1.2774, same for EUR/CHF at 1.4185 and EUR/GBP 0.8676 while EUR/JPY just misses the mark peaking at EUR/JPY at 117.80 just off the day's 117.90 peak. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0822 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR and the GBP have rebounded from previous lows but both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are still seen at risk from further losses. The EUR is finding support ahead of German and Italian parliamentary votes on Greece's rescue package as well as G7 finance minister talks and the release of the latest non-farm payrolls from the U.S., all later in the day. The GBP is waiting for UK politicians to sort out exactly how a hung parliament will work now that the Conservatives look unlikely to get an outright majority after Thursday's general election. European stocks have also rebounded from earlier lows but most bourses are still about 1% lower. This follows on from the sharp collapse in stock prices seen in the U.S. late Thursday both on fears over Greek contagion to other euro zone debtors and indications of technical hitches. The EUR is up at $1.2739 while the USD is up at Y92.10. But the GBP is still lower at $1.4721. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0822 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR implied volatilities spike as global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market volatility spurs cost of protection from adverse move in spot pair, says trader with foreign bank; 1-month ATMF at 8.80%/10.25% vs 8%/9% late Thursday. "The bidding has been strongest on the front-end with offers on the 1-week going as high as 12%," says trader. Notes, curve flattening a natural play as near-term \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market volatility may roil USD/INR for now, but INR bound to return to secular rally in coming months once global jitters conclusively resolved. Adds, risk reversals also well bid with 1-month 25-Delta RR's quoting at around 1.5%/2.50% from 0.75%/1.6% late Thursday. (bijou.george@dowjones.com) [ 05-07-10 0446ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100507e65700083\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
