\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Indonesia delays Samurai bond issue in market selloff\b0\par\par\uc2 533 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Mai 2010\par 06:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, background)\par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, May 6 (Reuters) - Indonesia postponed a planned $1.1 billion Samurai bond to the second half of 2010 as bond yields rose and the rupiah fell on investor fears over debt woes in the euro zone and the Indonesian finance minister's resignation.\par \par \uc2 But a senior central banker reiterated the country's strong growth prospects and played down a selloff in the rupiah.\par \par \uc2 Investors sold Indonesian stocks, bonds and the rupiah on Wednesday and Thursday, prompting the debt office to announce it would postpone its samurai bond issue to the second half of this year to avoid paying a higher yield.\par \par \uc2 Rahmat Waluyanto, head of the finance ministry's debt office, said the country would delay its planned samurai bond issue to the second half of this year.\par \par \uc2 "The yield increased 30 bps in a half day (yesterday). That was a significant rise. It shows a change in investors' perception on risks associated with Indonesia," he said.\par \par \uc2 "We will change our strategy. We will reschedule. We will postpone (the samurai bond issue) until the second half (of the year)."\par \par \uc2 In March, the debt office said Indonesia planed to raise $1.1 billion via a single Samurai bond issue that could be launched by the end of April or in early May. [ID:nJAK366519]\par \par \uc2 Another official at the debt office said Indonesia has raised its total debt issuance target for this year to 178.1 trillion rupiah ($19.60 billion) from the previous target of 176.2 trillion rupiah. The debt office also expects to raise a minimum 8 trillion rupiah from a retail bond issue planned for early August, said Bhimantara Widyajala, director at the debt office.\par \par \uc2 Separately, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's senior deputy governor said Indonesia's economy is expected to grow more than 5.7 percent in the second quarter from the same period a year ago and expand at an even faster pace in the third quarter.\par \par \uc2 "Economic growth in the second quarter will be better than the first quarter...and third quarter could be at around 6 percent," Darmin Nasution told reporters.\par \par \uc2 He played down worries over recent falls in the high-yielding rupiah <IDR=>, which lost over 1 percent earlier in the day, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent weakness was "normal".\par \par \uc2 "There is just a little weakness in the rupiah. We are still at a normal level and in the end the determining factor is the economic fundamentals. The impact on sentiment is only temporary."\par \par \uc2 His comments came after traders said \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the weakening rupiah amid growing concerns that Greece's debt crisis is spreading to other euro-zone countries. [ID:nSGE645029]\par \par \uc2 Investor sentiment was also undermined by Finance Minister Indrawati's decision to quit on Wednesday, as she had played a key role in improving the economy, traders said.\par \par \uc2 As of 0400 GMT, the rupiah was near 9240 per dollar. (Reporting by Adriana Nina Kusuma and Dicky Kristanto, Writing by Yoko Nishikawa; Editing by Sara Webb)\par \par \uc2 INDONESIA-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|IF|SI|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100506e65600091\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
