\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Indonesia Shares Likely Lower;2890 Support Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,241 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 2010\par 04:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely lower, weighed by weak IDR, falls across Asian markets after stocks in U.S. end sharply lower overnight on Euro zone debt woes, traders say; tip support at 2890 after ending down 0.2% yesterday at 2939.299. "I think the main index could fall by more than 1.5% today as bearish global markets may provide good reasons for further profit-taking after recent rally," says fund manager at Lautandhana Securities. Main index up 21% since start of 2010. Car maker Astra (ASII.JK), Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) may lead falls on profit-taking. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] Copper futures on Shanghai Futures Exchange sharply lower on spillover bearish sentiment overnight amid worries over eurozone economies, after S&P downgrades Greece bond ratings to junk status, says Shanghai Cifco Futures analyst Wang Zhouyi; adds copper may narrow losses later in day on technical buying, but Shanghai copper futures likely to face more downward pressure over next few sessions as worries over eurozone's economic health persist. Tips nearby support at CNY56,200/ton on chart-based cues; benchmark July contract now off 3.7% at CNY57,800/ton. (yue.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0213 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to push rates up by 100 bps in July after standing pat yesterday, as bank lending likely to accelerate in 2Q, while inflation pressures remain acute, says Capital Economics. SBV has no formal inflation target, isn't independent of government so will mostly share pro-growth position of authorities ahead of National Party Congress next January. Says Vietnam still needs to deal with rising inflation, which should peak around 10%-11% in mid-2010 before sustained slowdown towards 8% by year-end. "But for this to happen, the SBV will need to move soon to ensure that inflation expectations remain contained," house says. With GDP growth set to hit 6.5% for 2010, SBV likely to lift rates to peak of 10.0% in 3Q vs current 8.0% before easing in 4Q ahead of National Party Congress. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0212 GMT [Dow Jones] Even a surge in Australia's 1Q CPI won't force RBA to hike rates for a 6th time in 7 sessions when it meets next week, says \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  senior strategist Annette Beacher. "The RBA's inflation forecast is a little on the cautious side and they know that. Even though it's really a 50-50 decision, we think there is little harm in pausing at 4.25%." Adds "the inflation pressures are there, which is why I think cash rate needs to be in (the) restrictive zone at 5.25% by year end...just think it's time for a mid-cycle pause." (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead Kospi200 June futures down 1.9% at KRW225.45 on increase in risk aversion; foreigners net sellers both on cash, futures markets after VIX tops above 20% overnight following ratings downgrades on Greece, Portugal, says Lee Ho-sang at Hanwha Securities; "it's the day to manage risk. But I don't expect a further sharp pullback after today" as V-Kospi, volatility gauge like VIX, didn't jump as high as global peers, indicating somewhat stable sentiment. But further fall likely today if Asian peers retreat further. (soo-kyung.seo@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] Quick pace of Australian inflation won't prompt RBA to hike next week, but does suggest any pause period may be compressed, says Nomura Chief Economist Stephen Roberts. "I still don't think it's enough to make (the RBA) move in May given what's happening in global financial markets." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0202 GMT [Dow Jones] Greece-related risks for global economy and signs of stronger USD-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets like \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , South Korea may lead to an extended period of retracement and consolidation for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Standard Chartered says. Notes that Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  typically show poorer returns in 2Q, 3Q than in 1Q, 4Q, a trend that may be repeated depending on how global markets react to news and whether China moves on CNY front. "The fundamental picture may be robust, but risks remain (notably in Europe) and there is still the question of just how much of this positive picture is already priced in." (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0201 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP surges to 3-week high early, lifted by concerns over credit ratings downgrades of Greece, Portugal by \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2 . Pair last quoted at 44.63 in still light volume, up from 44.43 late Tuesday but off early high 44.75; support 44.50, resistance 44.80. "What we saw initially (PHP44.75 high) was just a knee-jerk reaction (to the Euro zone news)," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0201 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures higher on weak local stocks following news of credit ratings downgrade of Greece, Portugal, analysts say; Kospi last down 1.6%. 3-year yield down 5 bps at 3.61%, 5-year yield down 3 bps at 4.29%, June futures +13 ticks at 111.43. "This Greece news didn't come out of the blue, but this issue has triggered more doubts about the sustainability of the economic recovery around the globe," says \uc2 Woori Investment & Securities\uc2  analyst Park Jong-youn. Adds, "the (local) economy performed really well in the first quarter, but that'll be just as good as it can get." Expects bonds to extend gains in long term. Tips 5-year yield in 4.25%-4.32% range for day. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY up on broad USD strength, but likely capped on continued yuan appreciation hopes. Pair now at 6.8267 vs 6.8258 yesterday, tipped to hover around current levels rest of session. "We were surprised to see the market trading above the central parity as the onshore market is usually not affected by outside markets," says Guangdong-based foreign bank trader. Central parity set at 6.8264 vs 6.8262. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] April HSI futures down 1.7% at 20,893, suggests cash market expected to open below its psychological 21,000 support after \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  cut its credit ratings on Greece to junk. Contract ended at sizeable 247-point discount to cash yesterday, indicating near-term bias remains negative. April open interest down 8,845 lots at 59,798, as rollover expected to pick up ahead of April futures' expiry tomorrow. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0157 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares likely to extend gains after \uc2 Vinamilk\uc2  (VNM.VH) says its 1Q net profit +64.5% on year while Kinh Back City (KBC.VH) posts 1Q net profit of VND261.56 billion vs loss of VND59.92 billion year ago. "Buying sentiment is strong because most of the blue chips are expected to post solid earnings for 1Q," says trader with Vietcombank Fund. Tips key index in 536-540 range after rising 0.8% to end at 535.72 yesterday. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com) [ 04-27-10 2214ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100428e64s0001u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
