\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Creeps Higher; 9,005-9,025 Range Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,402 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 2010\par 03:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0144 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR creeping higher at 9,015 vs 9,008 late yesterday on short covering ahead of weekend, dealers say. "The dollar will likely be rangebound today, but biased higher on pre-weekend short-covering," dealer says; no sign of Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  buying pair this morning, after suspected $200 million \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  yesterday to support pair at 9,000. Pair tipped in 9,005-9,025 range for day. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0143 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI expected to stay in 22,000-22,200 range today, as modest rise on Wall Street unlikely to provide support to local market, while mainland developers set to be under pressure, after Chinese government raises downpayment for 2nd-home buyers to minimum 50% vs 40% in bid to curb property speculation, says Ernie Hon at ICBC International. "I think the Hang Seng Index will manage to stay above 22,000 level in the near-term, with commodity stocks likely a major driver." At pre-open, Cnooc (0883.HK) +0.7%, \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (0857.HK) +1.9%, Shenhua (1088.HK) +1.1% as energy plays viewed as safe havens from China tightening measures on property sector, expectations for medium-term USD weakness. Adds, more volatility expected from China markets ahead with launch of stock index futures today, but impact on HK unlikely to be major for now. HSI ended +0.2% at 22,157.82 Thursday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0137 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares down 0.2% at 8152.91 with financial plays (subindex down 0.4%) leading falls; losses in regional markets hurting sentiment while investors reluctant to chase prices higher on concerns China's economy overheating, says Andrew Teng, assistant vice president at Taiwan International Securities. Tips main index in 8100-8200 band for rest of day. Notes volume thin at NT$17.79 billion so far, though market breadth positive as buyers outnumber sellers; "the Taiex may launch a comeback later in the session." Fubon Financial (2881.TW) down 0.8% at NT$38.60, Cathay Financial (2882.TW) down 0.6% at NT$54.40 on China's potential tightening moves. (crystal.hsu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0131 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead Kospi200 June futures down 0.7% at 228.35 on heavy futures selling by local retail investors; futures prices falling more rapidly than spot prices. This spurring program selling, weighing on large-caps in cash market. "Investors are becoming cautious as recent sharp gains led the benchmark index to hit a new high. Also, they seem to be selling on news," such as \uc2 Moody's\uc2  upgrade of Korea's sovereign ratings, positive earnings for global leading firms amid concerns recent stock gains have already priced in most of positive news, says Jun Gyun at \uc2 Samsung Securities\uc2 ; but market mood remains positive, expects local stock market to recover, make gradual rise. Immediate support pegged at 228, near 5-day moving average. (soo-kyung.seo@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD falls slightly to 31.367 vs 31.375 late yesterday on continued TWD demand in anticipation yuan may rise soon, says foreign bank trader. But notes pair up from intraday low at 31.340, as TWD weighed by weakness in local shares (Taiex down 0.2%), narrower NDF discounts, stronger USD vs Asians. Tips pair in 31.350-31.400 band rest of morning. "There's a chance the pair may rebound later in the session if stocks drop further and foreign investors start selling local shares." Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may watch USD/KRW closely today to decide if it will allow TWD to continue to rise. (charmian.kok@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares likely to drift lower as investors continue to take profit ahead of the May 10 elections; benchmark PSEi tipped to slide toward 3250 support after closing 0.6% lower at 3278.84 yesterday, with index holding above 3300 resistance for most of trading session; "We saw profit-taking late yesterday and we might see follow-through selling early today. Investors are taking a breather after the market's recent gains, with factors such as the coming elections weighing on sentiment," says Oliver Plana, head of sales at Asiasec Equities; investors may focus selling on recent gainers such as property and banking stocks. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0117 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bond yields fall in line with Treasurys though domestic bonds likely to consolidate in Asian trade amid absence of immediate cues, trader says. Notes Singapore bond flows recently have been driven by SGD-strength buyers and external factors such as Greece. "I don't think there's a very big domestic catalyst to go either way at the moment." However, notes some switching out of longer-dated bonds into new corporate issuance, which may continue near-term. Thursday, 2-year yields ended down 4 bps at 0.49%, 10-year yield down 5 bps at 2.74%. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0104 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY lower on Nikkei weakness, but may rise back toward 93.50 vs last 92.91 if Reuters/University of Michigan index of consumer sentiment, due 1355 GMT, surprises to upside, says \uc2 Global Futures & \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Akira Maekawa. Upbeat retail sales on Wednesday increase "bullish view on consumer spending. Higher-than-expected numbers may heighten optimism on the U.S. economy." Dow Jones poll tips consumer sentiment index at 75 in April vs 72.5 in March. Eyes also on U.S. housing starts due 1230 GMT; expected up 6.1% in March vs down 5.9% in February. Pegs support at 92.50 rest of day. (miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0101 GMT [Dow Jones] Expect USD/SGD to trade in 1.37-1.38 range in Asia, pair going through some consolidation after rapid falls this week, trader says; "Now that the Chinese data is all over and done with people are starting to worry about Greece again." Greece edged closer to bailout yesterday, asking EU, IMF to begin "discussions" on aid, as country's cost of borrowing from private markets continued to rise, EUR tumbled. Fall in EUR overnight has pushed USD/SGD to higher end of this range. USD/SGD last 1.3770. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0100 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP may advance vs 44.355 late Thursday, tracking USD rebound vs EUR overnight, while banks expected to take profit on PHP gains after local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit fresh 20-month high (vs USD) yesterday. Pair may initially rise to 44.40 and head towards 44.50 immediate resistance; support eyed at 44.20. Still, traders think if local stocks gain following Wall Street's rise, pair unlikely to rise sharply; "U.S. stocks' gain overnight may provide some support for the peso," says local trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY may trade in current range as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to keep central parity steady, says Shenzhen-based local bank trader; "no one is interested in speculating in the market as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  shows no intention to change its stable yuan policy now." Central parity has been moving between 6.8259 and 6.8261 since start of April. Pair at 6.8260 late yesterday. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0052 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY, EUR/USD down on selling by non-Japan hedge funds as weak Tokyo shares (Nikkei now down 0.7%) fuel risk aversion, say senior dealers at major Tokyo banks. "Players are using declines in the Nikkei as an excuse to lock in gains" made early this week, says \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ senior dealer Mitsuru Sahara; adds "the euro's downtrend is intact" over near term. Also says worries remain that Greece may end up asking EU, IMF for aid, suggesting it could be "in a rush to seek funds", adding to cloudy outlook on some fiscally-troubled euro-zone nations. Tips EUR/JPY may fall to 125.00 vs last 125.82, EUR/USD may slip to 1.3500 vs 1.3540. USD/JPY down in tandem; support at 92.50 vs last 92.90. (miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com) [ 04-15-10 2144ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100416e64g00016\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
