\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: New Day's Low In EUR/USD\b0\par\par\uc2 1,205 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2010\par 11:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0945 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro's descent against the dollar is accelerating, hitting a new low for the day at 1.3528 weighed by nerves over Greece. On a technical basis, a sustained break below 1.3483 would be negative. Now 1.3535. (katie.martin@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0941 GMT [Dow Jones] Markit iTraxx indexes are moving wider after opening tighter Thursday as worries over Greece and other peripheral economies weigh. At 1035 GMT, the Crossover index is trading back above 400bps at 400/404bps, after opening at 389/393bps. Meanwhile, the Europe index is at 78/79bps, after opening at 75/76bps. (clare.conanghan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0918 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares end +0.5% at 2900.530 in moderate volume, helped by steady IDR, gains in many Asian markets, traders say; expect shares to consolidate tomorrow after gains in previous 3 sessions; tip index at 2890-2920 range. "Expectations that many companies will announce strong first quarter earnings on firmer rupiah and higher commodity prices kept the main index in positive territory despite sporadic profit taking by local funds," says trader at \uc2 Kresna Securities\uc2 ; index ends off intraday high of 2912.467. Among gainers: coal miner Bumi (BUMI.JK) +2.1% at IDR2,450, nickel miner Inco (INCO.JK) +5.1% at IDR5,150, while shipper Berlian Laju (BLTA.JK) +7.6% at IDR710. Profit taking hits gas and oil producer Medco (MEDC.JK) down 2.4% at IDR3,000. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0917 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys inch up in London trading as stocks come off their highs and safe havens get a boost on lingering worries over Greek finances. Thursday brings a raft of data, with NY Fed Empire Survey and jobless claims data, both due at 1230 GMT. Treasury international capital flows figures for February are due 1300 GMT. March industrial production figures are out 1315 GMT and Philadelphia Fed Manufacturing Index survey is out 1400 GMT. Speakers include Dallas Fed's Fisher, Richmond Fed's Lacker, St. Louis Fed's Bullard and Atlanta Fed's Lockhart, who speaks on the economic outlook. The 2-year note is near-flat at 99 30/32 yielding 1.032%, 10-year note up 6+/32 at 98 09/32 to yield 3.836% while 30-year is up 13/32 at 98 22+/32 to yield 4.706%. The June bund contract is up at 123.31 while the June gilt is up 13bps at 114.18. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com) Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 0917 GMT [Dow Jones] So much for the post-Bernanke EUR/USD bounce. Greek nerves are back in control, and the pair has plunged under $1.3550 from 1.3666 day's peak. (katie.martin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0917 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP extends losses, sliding to fresh 20-month low late, in line with rise of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; pair at 44.31 from 44.50 late Wednesday amid heavy trade; pair's slide tempered by profit-taking, says local bank trader. Adds pair may head further lower Friday as market eyes overseas cues. "We expect some profit-taking tomorrow but the general trend is for the peso to appreciate further." Strong support 44.20, resistance 44.50. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0916 GMT [Dow Jones] Sterling has sunk straight back down after early gains against the dollar, offering a hint that the $1.5525 area might be a bridge too far right now. USD has also jumped vs EUR as Greek nerves resurface yet again. GBP/USD at day's low of 1.5455. EUR/USD at day's low of 1.3555. (katie.martin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0915 GMT [Dow Jones] China GDP stronger than expected but imminent tightening is unlikely, says Dong Tao, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 's chief regional economist, non-Japan Asia; has been "a bigger than expected moderation in inflation (while) Beijing said the high growth has been achieved mainly by the base effect and government stimulus. (Also), industrial investment is still missing and the export recovery is strong but remains fragile." Adds one area standing out from an otherwise "Goldilocks situation" is property market, with prices still on rise; expects more tightening measures for sector, and a property market correction in 2H10 or 1H11 "remains the biggest downside risk for China's growth prospects." Says inflation pressure may emerge in 2H10 to trigger a rate hike. (Rosalind.Mathieson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0914 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia's benchmark KLCI turns positive late, ends +0.2% at 1338.83 on bargain buying in blue chips, after stocks trade in negative territory most of day. Construction plays (+0.5%) lead gains on hopes of more jobs after IJM (3336.KU) gets Sarawak contract, but tech stocks (down 0.8%) lower on profit taking after recent gains, says local dealer. OSK Research says keeps near-term bullish outlook, though says market appears to "struggle" around 1338, which represents neckline of modified head and should bearish reversal pattern in 2008; if 1338 breached, 1344-1358 downside gap will form next resistance zone. Support seen at 1334, then 1300. Decliners beat advancers 372 to 269. KNM (7184.KU) ends down 10.2% at MYR0.66 on failed buy out, Top Glove (7113.KU) down 1.1% at MYR12.60, CIMB (1023.KU) down 0.6% at MYR14.08; \uc2 Lafarge\uc2  (3794.KU) closes +4.9% at MYR6.80, IJM +1.9% at MYR4.95, Axiata (6888.KU) +2.4% at MYR3.81. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0912 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR well supported at 9,000 on suspected Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers say; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely bought total $200 million between 9,000-9,010, helps lift pair from 9,000 intraday low to 9,008 now, but still tad below 9,010 late yesterday. "I think Bank Indonesia will continue to support the dollar at IDR9,000 Friday to slow down its recent rapid fall so as not to hurt Indonesian exports," a dealer says; 9,000-9,020 range tipped for tomorrow. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0904 GMT [Dow Jones] Data over the last few days has confirmed that the global recovery is continuing apace with inflation pressures remaining subdued. As \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  says, "global economies are developing within a risk supporting sweet spot, defined by strong economic growth rates combined with low inflation data." (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0902 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds tick up as Greek bond yields surge, widening the spread Greece must pay investors to buy its debt instead of safe-haven bunds. There are concerns about possible delays to any aid plan for Greece, with the market also worried about potential debt troubles in other peripheral countries such as Portugal. June bunds are up 13bps at 123.27 and the benchmark 10-year bund is up at 101.11 to yield 3.116%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com) [ 04-15-10 0545ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100415e64f0006w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
