\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:China Property Sector Bears Watching-\uc2 Credit Suisse\b0\par\par\uc2 1,296 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2010\par 11:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0915 GMT [Dow Jones] China GDP stronger than expected but imminent tightening is unlikely, says Dong Tao, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2 's chief regional economist, non-Japan Asia; has been "a bigger than expected moderation in inflation (while) Beijing said the high growth has been achieved mainly by the base effect and government stimulus. (Also), industrial investment is still missing and the export recovery is strong but remains fragile." Adds one area standing out from an otherwise "Goldilocks situation" is property market, with prices still on rise; expects more tightening measures for sector, and a property market correction in 2H10 or 1H11 "remains the biggest downside risk for China's growth prospects." Says inflation pressure may emerge in 2H10 to trigger a rate hike. (Rosalind.Mathieson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0914 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia's benchmark KLCI turns positive late, ends +0.2% at 1338.83 on bargain buying in blue chips, after stocks trade in negative territory most of day. Construction plays (+0.5%) lead gains on hopes of more jobs after IJM (3336.KU) gets Sarawak contract, but tech stocks (down 0.8%) lower on profit taking after recent gains, says local dealer. OSK Research says keeps near-term bullish outlook, though says market appears to "struggle" around 1338, which represents neckline of modified head and should bearish reversal pattern in 2008; if 1338 breached, 1344-1358 downside gap will form next resistance zone. Support seen at 1334, then 1300. Decliners beat advancers 372 to 269. KNM (7184.KU) ends down 10.2% at MYR0.66 on failed buy out, Top Glove (7113.KU) down 1.1% at MYR12.60, CIMB (1023.KU) down 0.6% at MYR14.08; \uc2 Lafarge\uc2  (3794.KU) closes +4.9% at MYR6.80, IJM +1.9% at MYR4.95, Axiata (6888.KU) +2.4% at MYR3.81. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0912 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR well supported at 9,000 on suspected Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers say; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely bought total $200 million between 9,000-9,010, helps lift pair from 9,000 intraday low to 9,008 now, but still tad below 9,010 late yesterday. "I think Bank Indonesia will continue to support the dollar at IDR9,000 Friday to slow down its recent rapid fall so as not to hurt Indonesian exports," a dealer says; 9,000-9,020 range tipped for tomorrow. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0904 GMT [Dow Jones] Data over the last few days has confirmed that the global recovery is continuing apace with inflation pressures remaining subdued. As \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  says, "global economies are developing within a risk supporting sweet spot, defined by strong economic growth rates combined with low inflation data." (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0902 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds tick up as Greek bond yields surge, widening the spread Greece must pay investors to buy its debt instead of safe-haven bunds. There are concerns about possible delays to any aid plan for Greece, with the market also worried about potential debt troubles in other peripheral countries such as Portugal. June bunds are up 13bps at 123.27 and the benchmark 10-year bund is up at 101.11 to yield 3.116%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0846 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan interbank overnight rate climbs higher to nearly 14-month high 0.165% vs 0.164% yesterday on cash demand from brokerage houses, but rise limited as foreign banks have excess cash on hand, says foreign bank trader. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't accept (large amount of) NCDs/CDs purchase from foreign banks these days," she says, noting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has actively drained excess liquidity by accepting more purchase of NCDs/CDs from banks in previous weeks. Tips rate biased up as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to hike short-term rates gradually in short term. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0843 GMT [Dow Jones] Sweden's spring budget has an overly optimistic view of overall economic trends and domestic consumption, says Swedbank. Swedbank adds that hopes for declining unemployment could prove to be premature, noting "the government could have stimulated the economy somewhat more last year to limit the downturn." The bank adds that much in the budget had been announced in advance. The yield on Sweden's two-year bond trades one basis point lower at 1.51%, while the 10-year bond yield trades one basis point higher at 3.21%. (karl.bruze@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0842 GMT [Dow Jones] Despite recent strong rally, Mandiri Sekuritas still recommends buy on Indonesian government's 20-year FR52 as current 9.89% yield is still higher than fair yield of 9.80%; tips to take profit on 17-year FR45 (yield 10.05%) and 10-year FR31 (yield 8.64%; Offshore investors flocking to Indonesian sovereign bonds lured by relatively high yields, improving economic fundamentals and political stability. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0841 GMT [Dow Jones] The turmoil in Greece continues to keep a bid in the German bond market and "again and again" squeezes short positions in bonds, says \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2 . Adds as long as the Greek situation remains unresolved, it is hard to imagine a strong sell-off [in German paper]. "We do expect the situation to fade away at some point, but admittedly we have been disappointed many times and the timing is uncertain," \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  says. Adds getting over the May financing hump, with or