\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Hits More Than 2-Year Low; May Test 8,975\b0\par\par\uc2 1,595 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2010\par 03:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0140 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR slides further to 9,010, lowest level since Feb. 29, 2008, vs 9,030 late Friday, and may test 8,975 later today, on rising risk appetite around the globe, dealers say. Risk appetite getting boost from easing concerns over Greece's debt woes after the EU outlined terms of a potential aid package for the debt-strapped country over weekend. "The dollar could test lower if Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  doesn't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to slow pair's slide," a dealer says; adds profit-taking, however, could emerge and may drive pair higher later today; 8,975-9,025 range tipped. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0135 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead Kospi200 June futures down 0.2% at 226.60, off early high of 228.80 due to rise in local retail investors' futures selling amid sharp drop in USD vs KRW. "Such a rapid fall in the U.S. dollar against the Korean won seems to be giving investors an excuse to take profit in recent leaders, such as \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) and \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE), after their sharp gains," says Seo Joon-hyuck at Shinhan Investment Corporation; but expects still-solid gains in global stock markets to prevent drastic correction in Korean market. Tips 226, near 10-day moving average, as immediate support; if lead Kospi200 falls below 226, correction likely to last longer, deeper. (soo-kyung.seo@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD falls to 31.510 vs 31.615 late Friday on heavy foreign fund inflows, gains in local shares (Taiex last +0.6%), widening NDF discounts, says local bank trader. Tips pair in 31.450-31.500 band rest of morning. "The market also expects the yuan to rise soon, so a lot more foreign funds are flowing into the Taiwan dollar, which is more liquid and easier to trade than the yuan." But notes some investors buying USD at levels below 31.500 for pair on expectations \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may buy USD late in session to lift pair above 31.500. (charmian.kok@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0124 GMT [Dow Jones] IMM commitment of traders report shows net JPY short non-commercial positions vs USD up to 42,305 contracts in week to April 6 vs 30,866 contracts in week earlier, another indicator how fast investors bought USD/JPY on back of recovering sentiment on U.S. economic outlook as well as recently-rising U.S. yields. Investors say pair may target psychologically important 95 mark in weeks ahead, yet IMM data show investors excessively short on JPY, meaning they may need to adjust their portfolio - this resulting in slow rise in USD/JPY. Pair last 93.15; investors say cap at 93.70 for today, where Japan exporters' sell orders placed.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0123 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may extend Friday's gains after Wall Street's rally. Wing Fung Financial's Mark To tips index to be in 22,200-22,500 range today, with index benefiting from recent uptrend and rise in trading volume for overall market. "Follow-through buying interest is expected to support the local bourse, as Wall Street continued to extend its recent rally." Adds, H-shares could rise further near-term, as expectations of CNY appreciation will continue to support China plays. Also says, news euro-zone finance ministers Sunday laid out terms for a potential rescue package for Greece could offer some underlying support for market. Tips H-share index to be in 13,000-13,300 this week. HSI ended +1.6% at 22,208.50 Friday, H-share index +1.5% at 13,046. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB likely to remain on the upswing this week, even though action should be constrained by local market holiday for Songkran festival Tuesday-Thursday, Standard Chartered says, due to limited THB trading offshore. Notes Bank of Thailand likely to take latest outbreak of violence, which killed at least 21 over weekend, into account "and hence we see it keeping the benchmark rate unchanged in the next rate setting meeting" next week. "While we maintain our forecast of the next hike to take place in the June meeting, the latest political developments will clearly have a significant bearing on this decision," it adds. USD/THB last at 32.230 in thin offshore trade vs 32.210 late Friday. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] Any expectation China March trade deficit will take pressure off Beijing to change its yuan policy, will be quickly doused when China issues 1Q GDP data Thursday, says Credit Agricole; expects 1Q GDP growth at +11.5% on year (vs 4Q 2009 +10.7%), says while growth partly due to low base effect, "market may react to the headline figure and this may fuel CNY appreciation pressure." Strong March, 1Q figures to keep alive idea China needs to use wide range of tools to cool economy, including resuming yuan rise, house says. China expected to issue money supply, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves data ahead of GDP figures. Dow Jones poll tips March new yuan loans flat vs February at CNY700 billion, M2 growth 22.1% vs February's 25.5% growth. (jr.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0117 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB should rally given rising political violence in Thailand, but upside should be limited by lack of USD momentum in a day that should see gains in other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 . Notes "if it would have happened in any other day, it would have made the rally more significant...but today the USD's outlook is not that good after the news on the Greek bailout" with EU, IMF loans worth around EUR45 billion. Reckons BOT should be happy to see some correction in pair, after it fought recent slide with sustained USD-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; notes \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to clip upside only likely if THB selloff accelerates on capital outflows, a possible outcome as Thai stocks may take a big hit today. "The rosy scenario over the political situation that was priced in by the market was clearly over-optimistic. Money is leading for the exits." USD/THB last 32.230 in thin offshore trade vs 32.210 late Friday. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] Significant yuan revaluation would cause higher Chinese purchasing power and therefore should be short-term positive for commodities, resources, the Australian economy and Aussie equities, says Citi. Longer term, broker notes that stronger yuan could slow the Chinese economy, which would be negative for commodity prices, the Aussie economy and Aussie equities. Sees transport and gambling sectors as likely long-term beneficiaries of stronger yuan, while retail and consumer equities would stand to lose the most from a stronger yuan, given they source most of their products in China. That said, Citi expects yuan revaluation to be an orderly process, around 3% a year. S&P/ASX 200 up 0.7% at 4983.1. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0115 GMT [Dow Jones] BOK's rosier outlook for economy this year doesn't necessarily mean first monetary tightening could come earlier than expected, says \uc2 Korea Investment & Securities\uc2  economist Jun Min-kyoo; BOK today raises GDP growth forecast for 2010 to 5.2% from 4.6% previously, converging with market consensus for around a 5% rise, but "other conditions the BOK looks at when reviewing rates (such as global coordination) haven't changed. Stronger growth itself is not enough to prompt the BOK to move." Jun tips economy to grow 5.0% this year, 4.7% next year, first rate hike to come in September at earliest. Adds BOK's latest inflation forecast of 2.6% this year vs 2.8% earlier looks feasible. As host of G-20 summit in November, government, BOK have stressed need for global coordination in exiting stimulus measures; Majority view for rate hike timing is 3Q at earliest. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0112 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares likely higher, tracking Wall Street's rise Friday, amid easing concerns over Greece's debt problem; benchmark PSEi may test immediate resistance at 3280 after closing 0.4% lower at 3256.12 Thursday (markets were closed Friday for public holiday); next resistance eyed at 3300, support at 3200. "We'll probably trek higher, trailing overseas markets as (euro-zone debt) fears eased after the EU outlined terms of a potential aid package for Greece," says Astro del Castillo, managing director at First Grade Holdings. Blue chips such as PLDT (TEL.PH), \uc2 First Philippine Holdings\uc2  (FPH.PH) and \uc2 Globe Telecom\uc2  (GLO.PH), expected to lead market rebound following recent underperformance. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0105 GMT [Dow Jones] Benchmark 10-year JGB yield may hover in 1.360%-1.410% range this week due to mixed views on global economy, financial conditions, says Mitsubishi UFJ Securities chief strategist Jun Ishii. Notes 10-year yield may fall if speculation of China's revaluing yuan pushes yen higher, driving Tokyo shares downward, but could rise amid growing hopes that EU will bail out Greece; "the JGB market will likely waffle this week." 10-year cash bonds not yet traded. JGB futures now at 138.30, down 0.10. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com) [ 04-11-10 2140ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100412e64c0001d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw20