\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Indonesia Shares End Down 0.2% On Profit Taking\b0\par\par\uc2 1,411 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2010\par 12:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1001 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares end down 0.2% at 2880.969 in moderate volume, off all-time intraday high of 2908.361, on swift profit taking in most bank, telecommunication shares after recent rally, traders say; expect shares to consolidate tomorrow, with index likely in 2850-2900 range. "Profit taking by local funds reduced gains of the main index," says trader at Reliance Securities. Foreigners were net buyers of IDR363 billion worth of shares vs yesterday's IDR663 billion. Index still +18% since start of 2010. Leading falls: car maker Astra (ASII.JK) down 3.0% at IDR45,300, bellwether Telkom (TLKM.JK) down 1.8% at IDR8,200, while Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) off 2.2% at IDR8,750. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0946 GMT [Dow Jones] Pakistan shares trade lower; benchmark KSE-100 down 0.1% at 10,441.91 on lack of buying support from financial institutions, say market watchers. "The healthy gains recorded in the past couple of sessions have prompted domestic institutions to book some profits," says local dealer; notes, foreign funds bought $3.9 million worth of local stocks in Monday's session. Local analyst adds, "upcoming inflation numbers will be closely monitored and any decline there may ease short-term borrowing rates, thereby prompting local institutions to take fresh long positions on the availability of cheaper funds." Inflation rate widely expected to slow to 12.10% in March vs 13.04% in February; numbers expected within a week's time. \uc2 Pakistan State Oil\uc2  down 1.0% to PKR317.10, Oil and Gas Development down 0.5% to PKR131.20. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0943 GMT [Dow Jones] Interest-rate differentials are taking over from risk as the key driver of the financial markets, writes Martin Essex in Tuesday's Money Talks column. (martin.essex@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0934 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS slightly higher following gains in U.S. bond yields, but unchanged PBOC bill yield this morning caps gains. 3-year +1 bp at 3.19%-3.26%, 5-year +1 bp at 3.71%-3.78%. "The recent gains in U.S. rates and rate hike in Australia support further gains in swaps, but heavy selling pressure lingers as China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tends to keep secondary market yields stable," says Shanghai-based local bank trader. PBOC sells 1-year bills at 1.9264%, unchanged since Jan. 19. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0921 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  recommends reducing cash bond exposure to KPN and \uc2 Vodafone\uc2  (VOD), as it worries that cash outflows associated with upcoming spectrum auctions, or transmission rights from governments, could damage balance sheets and surprise credit investors. Rates both KPN and \uc2 Vodafone\uc2  at underweight. Says for investors wanting to retain exposure to the telecoms sector, its top picks in cash are Telefonica (TEF), \uc2 BT Group\uc2  (BT) and OTE (OTE). (art.patnaude@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0918 GMT [Dow Jones] US Treasurys move higher in London trading, consolidating the large move lower after the last few days of positive data. The outlook for economic growth is improving with some banks revising up their forecasts for 1Q GDP. However, fresh concerns hit Greece with disagreement over the rate to charge Greece for financial aid and with Greece indicating it would prefer not to tap the IMF for aid due to the Fund's stringent conditions. The FOMC is due to release its meeting minutes at 1800 GMT while ABC consumer confidence is due at 2100 GMT. The 2-year note is unchanged at 99 21+/32, yielding 1.164%, the 10-year up 8+/32 at 97 10/32, yielding 3.957%, with the 30-year bond is up 10/32 at 96 29/32, yielding 4.821%. Bunds and gilts fall with June bunds down 0.64 at 122.84 and June gilts down 0.70 at 114.29. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0917 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia shares end higher for 11th consecutive session; KLCI +0.2% at 1344.37 led by oil, gas plays following higher crude oil prices. Trader with local bank-backed brokerage says foreign funds inflow fueling gains (USD/MYR at 3.2100 late, 21-month low), "investors have been buying mid-cap stocks mainly...rotational plays like oil and gas have seen a lot of interest and chalking up very strong gains," dealer says. Adds, liquidity likely to sustain further gains; tips resistance at 1354, then 1360; support at 1341, 1338. Advancers beat decliners 375 to 339; Dialog (7277.KU) +4.5% at MYR1.17, Petra (7108.KU) +8.1% at MYR1.73, Kencana (5122.KU) +3.1% at MYR1.67, Tenaga (5347.KU) +3.4% at MYR8.38, Alam Maritim (5115.KU) +7.1% at MYR1.97. Hartalega (5168.KU) down 1.7% at MYR8.26, Pos Malaysia (4634.KU) down 2.4% at MYR2.86, UEM Land (5148.KU) down 4.6% at MYR1.45. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0914 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PKR lower at 83.95 late vs 84.28 late Monday as exporters cash out USD holdings, adding to pressure from strong stock market inflows. "Selling from exporters of about $25 million and inflows from foreign fund houses into equity markets of $10 million helped the rupee recover," says dealer. Adds, low demand from oil firms in absence of any import payment also helped local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, pair may recover as sharp slide would attract importer interest, adds dealer. Tips 84.00-84.10 range Wednesday. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0913 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR at 9,050 vs 9,065 late yesterday, off 20-month low of 9,035 on suspected Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers say; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected to have bought around $400 million between 9,035-9,045 to slow pair's slide; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't comment on specific \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but acting governor Nasution says today \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ready to act in market if necessary; it's occasionally spotted buying greenback in recent months after pair fell below 9,400; dealers tip 9,035-9,060 range Wednesday. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0908 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan's inflationary pressure, while gradually rising, remains moderate, meaning \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep benchmark interest rate at record low of 1.25% at least through 3Q, says Chinatrust Commercial Bank economist Alan Liao. Taiwan CPI +1.27% in March on year, marking 3rd straight month of increase but slowing down from +2.35% in February, while WPI +6.61% as global commodities prices rose while TWD remained subdued vs USD. "But (domestic) demand remains moderate so there's likely no chance of a demand push that will cause the wholesale prices to filter into consumer prices," says Liao, expects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue monitoring inflation, unemployment, tips 25 bps hike in September. (alex.pevzner@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0904 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR down at 3.2100 late, 21-month low, but off intraday low of 3.2060, vs yesterday's 3.2260; dealer with local bank-backed brokerage says pair continues to trend lower with sellers of dollar active in market after Easter break. No sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , means "smooth downward slide for the pair," says dealer. Adds, many brokers looking at revising targets for USD/MYR, some as low as 3.1000, on expectations Asia could outpace Europe, U.S. in economic recovery. "We expect the ringgit to continue to rise in the short term but there could be intermittent profit taking," dealer adds. Tips 3.2000-3.2200 range. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0903 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan average interbank overnight rate unchanged at 0.150%, as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to have slowed down pace of draining excess funds recently, judging from fewer sales of short-term paper (NCDs/CDs) Thursday, Friday, says local bank trader. "The rising pace of the overnight rate will not be as fast as recent weeks, unless the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decides to drain excess funds actively" by selling larger-than-expected amounts of NCDs/CDs in short term, he says. Taiwan average interbank overnight rate rose to 14-month high 0.163% last week. (mason.wu@dowjones.com) [ 04-06-10 0601ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100406e6460006g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
