\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Slides Further; May Test 9,080 Support\b0\par\par\uc2 1,524 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2010\par 05:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0438 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR slides further to 9,115 vs 9,165 late Monday on capital inflows into IDR-denominated assets, dealers say. "We saw fresh inflows into stocks this morning," dealer says; referring to 2.2% gain posted by Indonesian stocks. No signs of Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , say dealers; they expect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to let pair weaken to help reduce inflationary pressures; say 9,080 support may be tested, with resistance at 9,160. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0436 GMT [Dow Jones] Knee-jerk reaction to buy Australian bonds on morning FOMC statement fades and market could pull back more, says Stephen Nash, head of strategy for FIIG; "the market keeps on doubting the Fed's resolve to keep rates where they are. Every time they say the same thing, the market initially rallies and then that skepticism comes in." 3-year bonds recently at 94.73, flat after being up 7 ticks earlier. Also weighing on bond is heavy issuance of both government and semi-government bonds. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0433 GMT [Dow Jones] Macquarie says its 12-month Topix target remains 1,050, which discounts the slow recovery cycle to FY ending March 2012. Topix at 1,050 correlates with 19X house's FY ending March 2011 expected EPS of Y55, and 16X its FY ending March 2012 expected EPS of Y65. Expects FY ending March 2013 Topix earnings to return to their FY ending 2006-2008 high plateau of about Y75. "The Japanese bull is enjoying climbing the hill, but is still dependent on global markets to lead the way. Our base case remains domestic policy passivity." Nikkei 225 Stock Average now +0.6% at 10,787.46, +2.3% for 2010; Topix +0.5% at 943.04; +3.9% for 2010. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end tad higher as most investors remain tentative despite positive developments in overseas equities; benchmark PSEi ends 0.1% higher at 3088.05, volume heavy; "It looks like investors are uninspired by the Dow Jones (rally overnight), they are probably waiting for more convincing data that we aren't just out of the woods, but on the path to recovery," notes Jun Calaycay, trader with Accord Capital Equities. Tips market likely to stay in 3050-3100 range awaiting stronger leads. "The good news is that investors are finding no reason to liquidate their positions," adds Calaycay. Leaders include PLDT (TEL.PH), up 1.8% to PHP2,580; \uc2 Alliance Global Group Inc\uc2 . (AGI.PH), up 1.9% to PHP5.40. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0433 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY's profit-taking drop after BOJ easing, to as low as 90.02 from 90.40 on EBS, likely short-lived as market focuses on other USD-positives like continued strength in Japanese shares; Nikkei 225 off highs but still +0.6%. "As this was widely expected, the dollar-yen's decline was just driven by the sell-on-the-fact after players bought the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a rumor," says Nomura Securities senior dealer Hiroshi Maeba. Expects pair may stick to 89.00-91.00 range in longer-term. Support at 89.80 vs last 90.30. EUR/JPY support put at 124.00 vs 124.41. (miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0426 GMT [Dow Jones] India interest rate swaps lower after U.S. Federal Reserve gives markets some cheer by retaining relatively dovish language at policy meet, buying in local bond market also weighing on swaps while IMF Article IV report doing little to help as RBI seen staying wary of raising rates too fast despite growing inflation risk, says trader with foreign bank. 1-year OIS at 4.88%/4.92% vs 4.93%/4.96% last close, 5-year OIS at 6.96%/7.00% vs 7.02%/7.05% last close. "Despite every indicator warning the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that prices will be a problem, the language coming out of both the RBI and the government remains biased towards caution so far," says trader. Notes, receiving interest strong so far in session particularly on less than 1-year swaps, but large British bank with note-issuing powers in Hong Kong seen bucking trend by stepping in to pay INR16 billion in 1-month OIS. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0425 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold higher at $1,128.40/oz, up $2.50 vs NY close. Market "pretty directionless post-FOMC, much of the rise came before the announcement," says ScotiaMocatta precious metals director Peter Tse. Adds March traditionally quiet trading month for gold, other metals; tips gold to stay in $1,100-$1,140 range. "There are still too many uncertainties in Europe that could lead to a risk reversal that could quickly end this rally within the range," says Tse. (elisabeth.behrmann@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0422 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end down 1.0% at 510.85, with corporate investors selling on worries about possibility of monetary policy adjustment after CPI released later this week or next. "They fear the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may tighten monetary policy as March's consumer price index is expected to be higher than in previous years," says Nguyen Viet Hung, chief analyst at SME Securities; adds, leveraged investors also selling to reduce risks. Tips index in 505-510 range rest of week. Volume high with 51.3 million shares worth VND2.25 trillion traded. Among active shares, REE (REE.VH) +4.2% at VND50,000, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  (STB.VH) down 0.9% at VND22,900, Saigon Securities (SSI.VH) down 1.1% at VND88,000. (trong-khanh.vu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0421 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY rises moderately as market further trims expectations for CNY gains, taking note of stable fixing, recent strong comments from Beijing on keeping status quo --and despite a group U.S. Senators' call to classify China as \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator; onshore USD/CNY at 6.8260 vs 6.8259 late Tuesday and tipped in 6.8255-6.8265 band, offshore 1-year NDF at 6.6620/6.6660 vs 6.6560/6.6600. "Expectations for yuan appreciation are weakening a bit," says Shanghai-based trader at local bank; adds there's been recent BIS data showing CNY real effective exchange rate rose 4.1% in February vs January, one of largest sequential gains in recent months, which likely used by Beijing to counter U.S. claim it manipulates \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 . joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI extends gains, now +1.6% at 21,350.23, though size of gains not noteworthy, index breaches extremely narrow 21,000-21,300 band held for past 7 sessions (though break yet to be confirmed with a close above range). Technicals likely to attract more buyers as such a break usually implies extension of gains or losses (depending on direction of break); aided by likely short-covering or investors cutting position on longs. Technical break usually accompanied by higher volume; today's market volume picks up to HK$29.69 billion with about half an hour until midday close, already above HK$28.81 billion at midday yesterday. 42 of 43 blue chips now higher; those rising at least 3% include HK & China Gas (0003.HK), \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK), \uc2 Hang Lung Properties\uc2  (0101.HK), China Merchants (0144.HK), Esprit (0330.HK). (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0419 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD lower at 31.745 midday vs 31.820 yesterday, weighed by weakness in global USD, Taiex's 1.4% gain, says foreign bank trader. "Many foreign investors are expecting the New Taiwan dollar to rise more in the short term," judging from widening NDFs discounts, he says; tips pair to end below 31.800 in Taipei trade today, which would be first time since January 18. Tips pair in 31.700-31.800 band in afternoon. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0418 GMT [Dow Jones] BOJ likely chose not to extend durations for its lending facility as it "probably judged firms won't need a longer-term facility because the fiscal-year end is just a week away. Instead of trying to push the yield curve lower, the BOJ decided to provide funds to more firms," says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  chief Japan economist Kyohei Morita. But says this won't change recent trend for strong yen, falling prices, fragile economy; to put economy on more stable path "the government needs to cut the tax rate on firms, so they can resume active capital spending. That will close off the gap between supply and demand, a main cause of deflation." Says there's not much left that BOJ can do to combat deflation; "the BOJ has been doing what it should, or what it can." But government may not believe this action is enough, so pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely remain, he says. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-10 0038ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100317e63h00035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
