\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/THB Steady; Verdict Of Thaksin's Case Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,251 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2010\par 10:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0955 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB unchanged from 33.06-33.065 late yesterday. Pair drifts from 33.03 early low on buying by importers but upside limited by selling by exporters; foreign players mildly join selling force, says local dealer. "It's hard to predict what will happen. The market will probably be in idle mode while waiting for the (Thaksin asset case) verdict," he adds. Pair tipped 33.00-33.10 range. Supreme court will start reading its verdict in ousted-PM Thaksin's frozen asset case at 0630 GMT Friday. (PIY)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0953 GMT [Dow Jones] India's government must do away with dual imposition of Securities Transaction Tax, or STT, on investments into equity mutual funds in Friday's federal budget, says Dhirendra Kumar, chief executive of New-Delhi based Value Research, which provides fund analysis, industry data. "Investors pay STT on their investments into equity funds. However, when mutual funds invest that money in stocks, STT is again deducted from that money," adds Kumar. Says avoiding double taxation likely to attract more investments from retail customers, benefit industry in long run. India's mutual fund industry had total assets under management of INR7.59 trillion at end January, according to data from Association of Mutual Funds in India. (SHB)\par \par \uc2 0951 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares end down 1.2% at 2549.033 in moderate volume, weighed by weak IDR, falls in many Asian markets as concerns over Europe debt woes linger, traders say; expect shares to rebound Monday on bargain buying; tip resistance at 2570. Indonesian markets closed Friday for holiday. "Selling by foreign funds drove the main index lower despite sporadic buying by local institutional funds," says trader at Reliance Securities. Foreigners net sellers of IDR671 billion worth of shares. Astra (ASII.JK) down 1.5% at IDR36,250 on profit taking after gaining more than 4% in previous 5 sessions, \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) off 1.7% at IDR4,475, while Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) down 2.7% at IDR7,150. (EDH)\par \par \uc2 0947 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR maintains gains at 9,355 late vs 9,340 late Wednesday on month-end demand, as well as short covering ahead of long weekend, dealers say. "There's no sign of Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," dealer says; 9,325-9,375 tipped Monday; market closed Friday for public holiday. (IMS)\par \par \uc2 0941 GMT [Dow Jones] Sterling drops to the day's lows after data showed Business Investment in the UK fell at its sharpest annual rate in the data's 43 year history. GBP/USD hit a fresh month low of 1.5272 and EUR/GBP the day's high of 0.8830 for a half penny gain on the day. (GST)\par \par \uc2 0937 GMT [Dow Jones] The CBI distributive trade survey, out later than usual Thursday at 1245 GMT is of importance given that slowing consumer confidence has become evident in the major developed economies over the past few months, notes \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . Any signs that the UK consumer is slowing will just add to sterling's problems, says the bank, and with GBP/USD already below support at 1.5365 a weak survey could tip it over the edge toward an initial target of 1.5065. GBP/USD now at 1.53. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  is looking for reported sales at -5 vs -8 in January. (GST)\par \par \uc2 0931 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Indonesia's inflation rate in February likely to remain subdued, giving \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  leeway to keep policy rate flat, focus on spurring growth; Feb CPI likely +3.97% on year vs +3.72% in January, according to median forecast of 10 economists polled by Dow Jones, mainly due to lower comparative base; whereas on-month inflation likely to ease to 0.46% from 0.84% as basic food prices ease; Bank Indonesia deputy governor Sarwono says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "has no intention" to hike rate when it meets Thursday. Data due Monday around 0400 GMT. (IMS)\par \par \uc2 0926 GMT [Dow Jones] Greek government bonds remain under pressure mid-morning Thursday after \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  and \uc2 Moody's\uc2  ruminate about possible rating cuts, but regain some of the lost ground compared with the morning. At 0913 GMT 10-year Greek yield spreads over German bunds trade at 355 basis points, wider than 339 basis points at Wednesday's close, but off from highs at around 360 basis points in early trade. In market terms, Greek bonds yield 6.66%. (BEM)\par \par \uc2 0925 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia shares end flat, benchmark KLCI at 1270.78, off intraday high of 1277.66. Local dealer says market succumbs to profit taking late after trading in positive territory most of day due to good corporate earnings, Malaysia's better-than-expected 4Q GDP; tips index may test 1280 next, then 1288 in near-term as more strong corporate earnings expected late Thursday. Still, "underlying concerns over economic recovery and the unresolved sovereign debt issues in Europe may weigh on sentiment," dealer says, adding traded volumes moderate at 679 million shares as many investors cautious amid European woes. Advancers include Axiata (6888.KU) +6.6% at MYR3.73, Tan Chong (4405.KU) +2.3% at MYR3.17, Carlsberg (2863.KU) +2.6% at MYR4.68. Decliners are Tanjong (2267.KU) down 1.8% at MYR17.62, JCY (5161.KU) down 8.8% at MYR1.46, YTL (4677.KU) down 2.6% at MYR7.22. (KPL)\par \par \uc2 0922 GMT [Dow Jones] Swedish PPI rose sharply in January, but the gains were mainly driven by krona weakness and may thus be reversed in coming months, says SEB. "Even though the higher prices on consumer goods were larger than expected the weak krona is most likely the driving factor, which means that the upturn can be expected to be reversed in February and March, when the krona has more than reversed the weakness," it says. January PPI came in at +2.0% on month, +0.3% on year and against SEB's +1.5% and -0.8% forecast. EUR/SEK trades at 9.7459. (ANM)\par \par \uc2 0920 GMT [Dow Jones] Nordea says it sees better value in the BTP 4.25% March 2020 in Italy's bond auction Thursday than in the BTP 2% December 2012, as 10-year Italian bonds look "quite good" versus for example Spanish bonds. Senior analyst Jan von Gerich notes the relative stability of Italian bonds versus German peers in the past months compared to the market moves seen in the bonds of many other peripheral countries. "Although Italy has historically been one of the problem children of the euro zone in terms of public finances, this time Italy is actually facing public sector deficits smaller than those for the euro zone as a whole." (BEM)\par \par \uc2 0919 GMT [Dow Jones] SNB will remain vigilant in light of the long-term upward trend in CHF, \uc2 Credit Suisse\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Marcus Hettinger says. "A rise in the Swiss franc is similar to a tightening of monetary policy," he says, adding the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely remain in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  mode. Adds any Swiss rate hike--\uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  has 25-bp move in 3Q on the books--could be delayed if EUR/CHF falls further. Sees the pair's fair value at 1.40 in terms of purchasing power parity. Stands at 1.4631. (MGE) [ 02-25-10 0455ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100225e62p0007a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
