\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Up; But  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Eyed If Tops 9,450\b0\par\par\uc2 1,534 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2010\par 02:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR higher at 9,425 vs 9,390 late Monday on USD's firmness vs most rivals amid risk aversion in wake of concerns over sovereign debt of some European countries; EUR/USD likely eyed for cues. "But the dollar-rupiah remains within yesterday's trading range, reflecting that the market has priced in all factors that made the dollar well bid," a dealer says; no sign of Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but traders suspect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may sell pair if it rises above 9,450; 9,390-9,450 range tipped. (IMS)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0132 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI last down 0.8% at 1224.75, with finance stocks (subindex down 1.2%) leading falls, says dealer with local brokerage; "the broad-based selling is not unexpected as people are turning jittery with concerns over a sustained global economic recovery dogging sentiment." Tips immediate support at psychological 1220, followed by 1213 (61.8% Fibonnaci retracement). Volume moderate with 70 million shares worth MYR67 million traded, while market breadth negative with losers outpacing gainers 246 to 38. Among decliners, \uc2 Hong Leong Bank\uc2  (5819.KU) down 2.5% at MYR7.70, Public Bank (1295.KU) down 1.6% at MYR11.02, CIMB (1023.KU) down 1.0% at MYR12.10, EON Capital (5266.KU) down 2.2% at MYR6.52.(ECH)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI set to open lower mainly on likely \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) weakness with ADRs ending down more than 2% vs HK close, due to continued concerns over European debt issues. Still, some bargain hunting may emerge late in session given index already down 5.7% in past 3 sessions. HSI ends yesterday down 0.6% at 19,550.89, marking new 2010 closing low, down 10.6% year-to-date, with fears of China tightening overriding factor. Still, \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says "rather violent market reaction" unwarranted, thinks "Chinese authorities more likely to get macro policy right this time", economic hard lending unlikely. \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK), peers may stabilize on news CCB sets new loan quota for this year; Cnooc (0883.HK) may rebound aided by news of 3rd deepwater discovery in South China Sea, but brisk January property sales may not boost China Overseas Land (0688.HK) much.(RLI)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 China Investment Corp\uc2 .'s (CIC.YY) disclosure of its U.S. equity holdings shows the $300 billion sovereign wealth fund is less of a big bad wolf, more like Little Red Riding Hood's grandmother when it comes to investing. Holdings disclosed are only worth $9.6 billion, a small part of overall portfolio; within that, two investments dominate, in \uc2 Teck Resources\uc2  (TCK) and \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  (MS), both long known; otherwise, the portfolio is largely passive, with stakes in defensive companies, such as \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  (JNJ), \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2  (KO). "These could be the stock holdings of a 65-year old widow," says Michael McCormack, an executive director at Z-Ben Advisers. Disclosure may allay the fears of those who've worried CIC has more of a political than returns-based focus; as long as CIC remains such a grandmother in wolf's clothing, it may convince skeptics it really is just another long-term, prudent investor. See "HEARD ON THE STREET: CIC: Not Such A Scary Wolf" (AJP)\par \par \uc2 0119 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD tad lower at 7.7713 vs 7.7719 late Monday. Still, trader from Chinese bank tips firm near-term floor at 7.7705; says lingering concern about European sovereign debt will support USD. "If the HSI continues to fall, I won't be surprised if the U.S. dollar bounces back (against the Hong Kong dollar)." HSI lost 5.7% since Thursday. (ARP)\par \par \uc2 0101 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY gains a bit on Fed St. Louis President Bullard's remark U.S. discount rate to rise fairly soon in steps, on hopes of higher returns from U.S. assets, but some traders say buying coming more from major Japanese banks and insurance firms, which buying USD-denominated assets to adjust their long-term portfolios ahead of March 31 fiscal year-end. Senior trader at Japan securities house says big Japan names think USD/JPY below 90.00 is attractive level to buy USD, and "this has been one of the dollar-supportive factors in recent days." USD/JPY rises to 89.41 from 89.23 on Bullard, with resistance at 89.90, where Japan exporters' sell orders piled up. EUR/JPY rises in tandem with USD/JPY, with resistance at 122.70 vs last 122.13.(MIN)\par \par \uc2 0059 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may consolidate with downward bias, tracking overnight Wall Street weakness, lingering domestic concerns about tighter reins on mortgages. "It's going to be consolidation (mode) before the week-long Lunar New Year holiday, and I don't see much possibility of a rebound," says Jacky Zhang, analyst from Capital Securities. Pegs Shanghai Composite Index in 2900-3000 range after last ending down 0.1% at 2935.17. Weak demand may cause \uc2 China First Heavy Industries\uc2  (601106.SH) to fall below its initial public offering price on its Shanghai debut today, amid poor performance of other recent debutantes. Shenzhen Composite Index +0.2% at 1099.15. (FSE)\par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely to rise vs 32.195 late yesterday, on possible foreign outflows after Commercial Times reports \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may pay attention to around NT$60 billion in foreign money idling in TWD accounts after foreigners net sell NT$130 billion of local shares (rest already remitted out) in past 2 weeks, citing bank sources. "Fund repatriation may accelerate ahead of the Lunar New Year holiday." Still, expects possible USD rise to be limited by exporters' selling for settlement needs, expected late-session \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in favor of TWD. Tips pair in 32.100-32.250 band for day.(CHY)\par \par \uc2 0054 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead March JGB futures erase earlier gains, last flat at 139.10, after Nikkei pares earlier losses; but demand for JGBs may get boost throughout yield curve later if MOF's Y600 billion tender of 30-year notes (result due at 0345 GMT) draws solid demand; that result expected as 30-year yield at last 2.315% should "attract steady and dip buying from domestic real money" players such as life insurance companies interested in locking in higher yields, says RuiXue Xu, debt markets strategist at \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2 . Adds, another reason to expect smooth result due to buying by life insurance companies is that they have in past tended to channel funds out of foreign bond holdings into JGBs toward fiscal year end in March; says "we think this trend will hold," as recent MOF data show "life insurance companies sold Y390 billion in foreign bonds" in January.(ATM)\par \par \uc2 0054 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to fall after ending flat at 7215.88 yesterday, on losses in U.S. shares, with foreign investors expected to mark 13th straight session of net selling ahead of Lunar New Year holiday starting Saturday, say KGI Securities trader Randy Chang. Still, losses likely limited as "the government funds will continue supporting the main index to at least maintain (some) market sentiment before the bourse closes Thursday." Says island's January export +75.8% to US$21.75 billion, biggest growth in over 33 years, as well as tech companies' healthy January revenue to also help bolster market. Tips main index in 7100-7250 band. (JEH)\par \par \uc2 0054 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds likely to fall amid expected sluggish trade as participants may continue to pare holdings in final 2 trading days before Lunar New Year holiday, and ahead of auction of 20-year bonds tomorrow, says foreign bank trader; "most traders would prefer to retain cash rather than having to worry about their portfolios over the week-long holiday." But 2-year bonds likely to rise on expected foreign investor buying on Commercial Times report \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may pay attention to around NT$60 billion of foreign money idling in TWD accounts after net selling of over NT$130 billion in local shares (rest already remitted out) in past 2 weeks, citing bank sources. Tips most active 10-year yield in 1.3900%-1.4100% band for day vs 1.4000% last finish, 20-year in 1.9300%-1.9500% vs 1.9300% in when-issued trade; 2-year yield in wide 0.1000%-0.3000% range vs 0.3250%, 5-year at 0.8800%-0.8950% vs 0.8849%.(CHY)\par \par \uc2 0053 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP likely higher on expectations of more fund repatriation as investors remain risk averse following overnight losses in U.S. stocks; pair likely to head towards 46.70 initial resistance after settling at 46.59 late Monday; support eyed at 46.40. Still, pair unlikely to move sharply; "we didn't see any big moves in major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  while the Dow was down overnight," says trader with large local bank. (SFL) [ 02-08-10 2033ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100209e6290001a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
