\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesian, Philippine c.banks seen supporting  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 153 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2010\par 09:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Jan 26 (Reuters) - Central banks in \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Philippines \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday in an effort to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  which took a hit after China implemented a rise in bank reserve requirements to curb bank lending, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso <PHP=> ended local trading at 46.595 per dollar, 0.8 percent weaker than the previous close.\par \par \uc2 "We got BSP (the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) well placed on the offer," said a trader in Manila.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> fell to 9,440 per dollar -- down about 1 percent from Monday's close -- after hitting its intraday high of 9,280.\par \par \uc2 "We suspect \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling at high today at 9,440," said a Jakarta-based trader. (Reporting by Kevin Yao; Editing by Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (URGENT)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|IF|PHD|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100126e61q000i7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
