\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Lower; May Head Down To 9,100\b0\par\par\uc2 1,479 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 2010\par 02:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR lower at 9,150 (pair's lowest level since September 2, 2008) vs 9,220 late Friday tracking broad USD weakness, dealers say; Bank Indonesia not yet spotted buying in market to stem IDR's rise, but may enter market later today if pair continues sliding close to IDR9,100. "The dollar's slide is quite fast, which may trigger Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," dealer says; 9,100-9,200 day range tipped. (IMS)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0135 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures sharply higher in early trade amid fading rate hike prospects for 1Q, analysts say; 3-year yield down 10 bps at 4.26%, 5-year yield down 8 bps at 4.83%, March futures +39 ticks at 109.39. "Expectations for a rate hike in the first quarter have been significantly reduced after the the vice finance minister started sitting in for the BOK meetings last week, while a rather weak U.S. jobs report is further sapping rate hike hopes," boosting bonds, says \uc2 Daewoo Securities\uc2  analyst Yoon Yeo-sam. Notes, foreign investors buying futures aggressively after breach of key 109.25 resistance. Says bond futures may rise to as high as 109.50, puts support for 5-year yield at 4.77% today. Says rate hike in 1Q unlikely, expects BOK tightening to start in 2Q. (MJL)\par \par \uc2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares advancing, now +0.5% at 8318.18 on gains in all sectors, steel titles in particular on expectation steel producers to raise prices as demand should grow along with implementation of infrastructure projects around globe. Steel subindex gains 2.2%, with sector giant China Steel (2002.TW) up 1.4% to NT$35.35. Financials up 0.7% altogether, with Cathay Financial (2882.TW) rising 1.0% to NT$61.0 after reporting strong 2009 net profit. "But it would bit difficult to rise much after recent gains, especially when momentum from foreign investors is declining ahead of the National Stabilization Fund's meeting...there is no reason for them to buy when the Fund seeks to offload it shares," says David Li, trader at Daiwa-Cathay Capital Markets. Stabilization Fund widely expected to slowly pare holdings this year. For rest of session, Li expects Taiex to trade in 8280-8340 band. (PEL)\par \par \uc2 0125 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD down at 31.798 vs 31.880 late Friday, tracking weaker global USD in New York session Friday after disappointing December U.S. jobs report, says local bank trader; adds gains in local stocks (Taiex last +0.5%) also weighing. Still, thinks pair's downside likely limited due to chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  support; "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely step in later," to help stem sharp TWD rise. Tips pair in 31.700-31.880 band. (ABP)\par \par \uc2 0123 GMT [Dow Jones] Disappointing U.S. non-farm payrolls data Friday may set a weaker tone for USD ahead of Thursday's ECB meeting, says DBS. Notes poor numbers should hurt U.S. rate hike expectations, and keep USD as a funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for carry trades; "in December, the USD staged an impressive recovery after the payrolls data posted strong upside surprises...(conversely), a difficult U.S. jobs recovery should keep the hawks at bay." Reckons latest data on market positioning - indicating market net short EUR/USD - may contribute to upward trend in pair. EUR/USD last 1.4480 vs 1.4414 late Friday in NY trade.(DRB)\par \par \uc2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD steady, last at 7.7558 vs 7.7560 late Friday. Local trader says pair likely to trade in 7.7550-7.7570 range for most of this week, as disappointing U.S. non-farm payrolls in December (down 85,000 vs flat in estimates) may encourage foreign funds to stay parked in HK near-term. "Some investors seem to get more cautious on taking more risks following the jobs data. They are awaiting more cues from (U.S.) retail sales data (due Thursday) before making bigger bets. Until then, the Hong Kong dollar should be well supported around current levels." (ARP)\par \par \uc2 0118 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Macquarie Bank\uc2  says zinc looking overpriced after strong rally last week. "While the next three to four months should see zinc continue to trade at elevated levels, taking a 6- to 24-month view, zinc is looking increasingly overpriced," says Macquarie. Zinc isn't as supply-constrained as copper, was one of the first markets to move into oversupply in the last boom, has seen more significant stock build during downturn than copper. "The outlook for increasing marginal costs of Chinese supply is supportive but, even on the most bullish assumptions, only at levels that are still 20% below current prices," says Macquarie. LME 3-month zinc trading at $2,610/ton, +3.6% vs PM kerb. (EFB)\par \par \uc2 0108 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may drift in narrow range, 22,000-22,600 likely short term, given lack of convincing cues one way or other, says Ben Kwong of \uc2 KGI Asia\uc2 . "The U.S. job report is weaker than expected, but that lowers the expectations of a rate hike... in China, while the government continues to take tough stance in controlling property prices, the stock futures news, on the other hand, is positive for sentiment." Chalco (2600.HK) set to rise on second alumina price hike in a week; mainland developers likely to lag more in short term as China steps up sector tightening. HSI ended +0.1% at 22,296.75 Friday, finishing week +1.9%.(RLI)\par \par \uc2 0105 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares likely to surge as much as 5% after China says late Friday Cabinet has approved launch of stock index futures, a trial program for short selling, margin trading of stocks. Shanghai Composite Index may rise to as high as 3350-3400 region after ending +0.1% at 3196.00 Friday, says Capital Securities analyst Jacky Zhang; "it will certainly push investors to buy more large-cap stocks such as financial companies before the launch of the stock index futures." Adds China's strong December trade also boosts sentiment, but says upside likely limited as real estate firms likely to remain weak after China says will step up efforts to keep hot money inflows from reaching property market. Brokerages may get a boost on expectations of actual short selling, margin trading adding to market liquidity after trial program complete. Shenzhen Index ends +0.7% at 1187.86.(JIN)\par \par \uc2 0105 GMT [Dow Jones] Acting chief economist at ANZ, Warren Hogan, says the pace of increase in Australian job ads is helping to stabilize the unemployment rate, balancing out strong population growth. Australian job ads rise 6.0% in December from November, fourth solid monthly rise in the last five months. Hogan says survey points to another month of strong official employment data, due Thursday. ANZ expects to see a further 30,000 jobs created across the economy in December. Hogan says data suggest the peak in unemployment will be far below the originally feared rates of 7% to 8%. Strong labor market is crucial if RBA is to raise rates a further 25 bps in February. (JEG)\par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY may fall slightly following higher-than-expected December exports, but downside limited as trade surplus declined from November. Central parity tipped around 6.8276 vs 6.8279 Friday. "We may see more losses in onshore and offshore forwards, but the spot market will be quiet as the steady yuan policy won't be changed while the declining trade surplus still concerns the government," says Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. China December Exports +17.7% on-year vs 5% expected. December trade surplus at $18.43 billion vs $19.9 billion expected. Pair traded at 6.8275 late Friday. (WYL)\par \par \uc2 0053 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Macquarie Bank\uc2  maintains bullish copper view in short term; thinks any selloff following recent rally and/or in face of index re-weighting over the next five business days a buying opportunity. Major commodity indices such as DJ-\uc2 UBS\uc2  Commodity Index and related sub-products started annual reweighting Friday, to continue this week. "Despite the market today being in surplus, the short-term outlook for the drivers of the rally in the price over the past six months remain intact - ie, booming Chinese demand, continued improvement in world ex-Chinese demand and strong fund demand," says Macquarie. Over medium term, copper remaining constructive, fundamentals likely to catch up with prices; that said, recent rally has changed risk/reward ratio in copper price. LME 3-month copper closes down $70 vs PM kerb at $7,460/ton. (EFB) [ 01-10-10 2036ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100111e61b00010\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
