\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Lower Late: May Head Higher Fri\b0\par\par\uc2 1,127 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2010\par 09:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0854 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR lower at 9,235 in late session vs 9,275 late yesterday on capital inflows, but suspected Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  helps slow pair's slide, dealers say; \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suspected of buying around $10 million earlier today, with acting governor Darmin Nasution saying IDR's recent appreciation "quite fast." "Profit taking against the rupiah may emerge Friday ahead of the weekend, which will lift the dollar higher," says local dealer; 9,220-9,280 range tipped tomorrow. (IMS)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0848 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/HUF is trading in a tight range Thursday, amid lackluster trade, traders say. EUR/HUF is at 269.0 versus 268.50 earlier in the morning, and 268.70 Wednesday afternoon. \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  notes negative news from Iceland leaves HUF unfazed, unlike previous occasions. Raiffeisen says even reviving Greek fiscal worries have failed to depress HUF, unlike at the end of 2009. Both expect tight EUR/HUF trading range to persist, Raiffeisen sees the cross trading between 267.25 and 279.85 Thursday. (MAF)\par \par \uc2 0846 GMT [Dow Jones] The Turkish lira and bonds are stronger as investors take profits in the stock market and shift to bonds and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. USD/TRY trades at 1.4730 from 1.4760 Wednesday, while the benchmark August 3, 2011 bond trades at 8.86% from 8.94% Wednesday. Analysts say any developments on a new IMF loan deal and US jobless claims data due 1330 GMT will be important for direction. They forecast TRY to trade between 1.4650 and 1.4820 and the benchmark bond to trade between 8.83% and 8.9%, in the short term. (IHA)\par \par \uc2 0845 GMT [Dow Jones] French government bonds OAT April 2018 and October 2023, both on offer along with two other series, are likely to be issued in response to dealer demand, and should go well, say \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  strategist Huw Worthington and Cagdas Aksu. The October 2019 and April 2041 bonds, which are also on offer, are looking "good value" versus their German equivalents, they say. "Overall we expect the auctions to go relatively well." Auction results are due at 0950 GMT. (BEM)\par \par \uc2 0844 GMT [Dow Jones] The sharp improvement in Hungary's January-September current account will continue in 4Q, a positive for the HUF provided a benign external risk environment, says ING. A 2009 balance of payments surplus will support further cautious rate cuts in January/February, before the expected pause around the April parliamentary elections. Notes imports will eventually bounce back once domestic demand recovers, but with a tighter fiscal policy this shouldn't lead to an excessive current account deficit widening and should also be offset by a recovery in exports. (MAF)\par \par \uc2 0843 GMT [Dow Jones] The Swiss franc may well have strengthened to a post-SNB-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  high against the euro overnight; however, Nick Hasting's \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Focus argues that the franc may start to self-correct without the SNB's help. (GST)\par \par \uc2 0840 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP lower at 45.83 vs 46.01 late Wednesday, retreating to fresh 16-month low in absence of new market direction; pair may continue range trading Friday, says local bank trader, may stay within 45.75-46.00 band. "I would prefer to buy on dips," adds trader. (RSA)\par \par \uc2 0838 GMT [Dow Jones] The pound is under pressure again, but this time from politics and not deficit worries. On Wednesday two Labour back-benchers called for a secret ballot on PM Brown's leadership. Standard Bank's Steve Barrow says the news has so far done only collateral damage to the pound but there could be a lot more damage ahead as the political fighting, not to mention infighting, escalates ahead of the upcoming election, which much be held by June 2010. GBP/USD has just printed a fresh one week low of 1.5936, EUR/GBP a one week high of 0.9024. (GST)\par \par \uc2 0833 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  expects good demand at France's OAT auction Thursday, despite the large amount, EUR7.5 billion to EUR9 billion, to be issued. The Oct. 2019 and April 2041 OATs, both among the OATs on offer, are cheap relative to the curve, it says, adding "we also like the 30-year OAT relative to [Dutch government bond] DSL." Auction results are due at 0950 GMT. (BEM)\par \par \uc2 0831 GMT [Dow Jones] Newly installed Japanese Finance Minister Naoto Kan makes his presence felt early with comments on his first day in the job that he would like to see a weak JPY. He even went as far as to quote a level, saying that many Japanese firms were in favor of the dollar being around Y95.00, USD/JPY spiked some 60 sen on his comments to the day's high of 92.88, it now trades at 92.73. (GST)\par \par \uc2 0828 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts open lower Thursday ahead of the BOE rate decision, due 1200 GMT. Although, no change is expected from the meeting and underperformance is likely to continue, thinks Peter Schaffrik, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  strategist. "Something is rotten in the state of Great Britain. Again, government credibility is at the heart of the current market fears," he says. Calls for a secret leadership ballot hardly helped on Wednesday and after Pimco's negative comments on the asset class earlier in the week, gilts have few positives. Elsewhere, US jobless claims are due at 1330 GMT but Friday's US nonfarm payrolls is this week's main focus. The gilt yield curve steepens, with the 2-year/10-year benchmark spread widening to +279.1 bps from Wednesday's +277.5 bps. The March gilt futures contract is currently down 0.30 on the day at 113.74. The 10-year benchmark gilt down 0.30 n the day at 103.134, yielding 4.085%. (NKA)\par \par \uc2 0825 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR slips to new 1-year low as exporter selling hits spot, forwards market hard, exporters using every support to dump dollar exposures, says foreign bank trader. Pair at 45.69 vs 45.81 earlier, 45.86 late Wednesday, while 1-year forward premium at 1.06 vs 1.14 late Wednesday "It's a one-way street. There's no telling where this could end," says trader. Notes, pair had nipped above key 45.80 support earlier in session, but large German bank set ball rolling by dumping dollars sparking widespread bout of selling. Adds, pair may find support at 45.60 within session, but may continue to drift lower to test 45.00 over next few sessions. (BIJ) [ 01-07-10 0354ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100107e61700061\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
