\par\par \b \uc2 Bank  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  Likely  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  To Support Dollar - Dealers\b0\par\par\uc2 128 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2010\par 09:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Tuesday to support the dollar after the rupiah strengthened sharply, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia likely bought a total of $200 million over the morning and afternoon sessions between IDR9,290 and IDR9,320 after inflows to local high-yielding assets boosted the rupiah, the dealers estimated.\par \par \uc2 The dollar regained lost ground, rising to IDR9,325 late in the Asian day from an intraday low of IDR9,290, its weakest level in around three months. The dollar fetched IDR9,340 at the end of domestic trading Monday.\par \par \uc2 -By Reuben Carder, Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 3983 1277; Reuben.Carder@dowjones.com [ 05-01-10 0908GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100105e615000nr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
