\par\par \b \uc2 Bank Indonesia Likely Sells $10M At IDR9,505-IDR9,515 -Traders\b0\par\par\uc2 185 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Dezember 2009\par 02:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--The Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely sold the U.S. dollar in early trade Wednesday to prevent the rupiah from weakening sharply, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia likely sold around $10 million between IDR9,505 and IDR9,515 as the dollar's overnight gains and dollar buying by local investors to fund year-end payments pushed that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher.\par \par \uc2 The dollar retreated from a high of IDR9,515 following the suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and was trading at IDR9,510 around 0125 GMT. The dollar fetched IDR9,495 in late Asian trade Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "It looks like they're trying to bring (the dollar-rupiah level) back below IDR9,500," one trader at a foreign bank said.\par \par \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said, was relatively small as liquidity is thin ahead of the holiday period.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the dollar is likely to trade between IDR9,490 and IDR9,520 for the rest of the day.\par \par \uc2 -By Reuben Carder, Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 3983 1277; Reuben.Carder@dowjones.com [ 23-12-09 0139GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091223e5cn00020\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
