\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Indonesia Shares End +0.3%; Likely Rise Monday\b0\par\par\uc2 1,449 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Dezember 2009\par 10:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0941 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares end +0.3% at 2474.833 in light volume, off intraday low of 2458.148, tracking gains across Asian markets after stocks in U.S. end higher overnight. "Foreign buying helped improve overall sentiment here despite selling by most local retailers ahead of long weekend," says trader at Valbury Securities; expects shares to gain Monday on year-end window dressing; tips resistance at 2500. Indonesian markets will be closed Thursday and Friday for Christmas holiday. Among gainers: gas distributor Gas Negara (PGAS.JK) +2.0% at IDR3,825, cement maker Indocement (INTP.JK) +3.1% at IDR13,300. Profit-taking hits bellwether Telkom (TLKM.JK) down 1.0% at IDR9,600. (EDH)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0937 GMT [Dow Jones] India interest rate swaps trade lower as market pushes back expectations of potential RBI tightening moves, but no major moves in market as many traders avoid taking on heavy positions in holiday season, says state-run bank trader; 1-year OIS at 5.02%/5.06% vs 5.04%/5.08% last close, 5-year OIS at 6.83%/6.87% vs 6.86%/6.90%. "Rates could well (move) further down as rate hikes now seem relatively further away, but the volumes just aren't big enough to unwind at one go without taking that impact cost," says dealer. Tips rates to slowly drift lower, 1-year OIS may slip by 10 bps over next few sessions unless new cues come in. (BIJ)\par \par \uc2 0934 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia shares end steady, with benchmark KLCI +0.11 points at 1,260.53 in thin volume of 338 million shares traded. Dealer with local bank backed brokerage says index traded within tight range but mainly in negative territory before mild bargain hunting pushed some heavyweight stocks higher late. Market breadth marginally negative with 282 decliners vs 268 advancers. "The 1,260-1,262 levels (50-day moving average) (turned out) to be a key resistance today... charts indicate further weakness," dealer adds. Tips 1,250-1,265 range tomorrow. Advancers include Hai-O (7668.KU) +4.7% at MYR7.85, Proton (5304.KU) +3.7% at MYR3.95, SP Setia (8664.KU) +2.2% at MYR3.68. Decliners were EON Capital (5266.KU) down 2.9% at MYR6.80, IJM (3336.KU) down 2.9% at MYR4.32, Gamuda (5398.KU) down 1.9% at MYR2.61. (KPL)\par \par \uc2 0929 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB higher at 33.34 vs 33.255 late yesterday amid broadly positive USD tone. Buying from interbank players helping propel USD, but Bangkok-based dealer says "there are still quite a lot of exporters trying to sell (USD) before year-end." Adds exporters offloading USD at 33.33-33.34 today. Pair remains biased upwards though some expect USD strength to be short-lived; 33.30-33.40 range tipped tomorrow. (LHM)\par \par \uc2 0926 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bond prices will likely plunge tomorrow as government surprises market with larger than expected 1Q issuance of NT$190 billion (expected NT$150 billion), out of which NT$70 billion is in 20-, 30-year bonds, for which demand may come only from insurers, says local bank trader, adds amount for 20-year bond (NT$40 billion) also larger than expected; 30-year bond sale is first since 2004. "I don't think insurers will be able to digest this much and this will be reflected in the yields," he says; expects yield curve to steepen at long end. Yield on benchmark 10-year bond to range at 1.50%-1.55% vs today's close 1.4822%, 20-year may jump to 2.10%-2.17% vs 2.0790%. (ABP)\par \par \uc2 0922 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD could slide toward 0.85 and NZD/USD 0.67 says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  but such moves will offer a buying opportunity. That's because in the New Year the bank says it expects more risk appetite to favor the Pacific Rim \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and carry trade strategies. AUD/USD now trades at 0.8755, NZD/USD at 0.70. (GST)\par \par \uc2 0922 GMT [Dow Jones](An item at 0551 GMT misstated the date when the index last ended higher, while the headline also misstated the index highs. The correct version is as follows:) Taiwan shares settle 0.6% higher at 7,901.50, best settlement since June 20, 2008's 7,902.44, led mainly by electronics titles' 1.0% gain as investors bet shipment growth to remain strong through Lunar New Year holiday, says KGI Securities trader Randy Chang. Index hit as high as 7,910.88, but failed to rise further in part because most foreign investors already on holidays. "People are buying hopes...for a better 2010." Hon Hai (2317.TW) gains 2.9% to NT$144.5 on biggest volume since late October as investors expect company's 2010 profit growth to accelerate with three-way merger of its units Innolux, Chi Mei and TPO. Nanya Technology (2408.TW) limit up 7% at NT$24.90. Daiwa Securities SMBC analysts Jae Lee and Pranab Kumar Sarmah say Nanya among their favorite DRAM names on expectation cyclical recovery in memory-chip industry will continue throughout 2010. For rest of year, Chang tips 7,800-8,000 band. (PEL)\par \par \uc2 0919 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR slightly higher late at 9,500 vs 9,495 late yesterday, but off 9,515 intraday high after suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , late USD selling. Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  spotted selling total of $15 million-$20 million over both morning and afternoon sessions to cap rise in pair on broad USD strength, year-end USD buying by local corporates; local exporters also eyed offloading excess USD in afternoon session, helping pair ease from highs. Underlying tone to stay bid tomorrow ahead of trading holiday Friday, but Bank Indonesia likely guard upside, limiting pair in narrow 9,490-9,510 range, dealers say. (RLC)\par \par \uc2 0917 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia consumer prices fell 0.1% on year in November on high base effect vs median 0.2% drop expected in Dow Jones poll of 10 economists. "The CPI numbers are very much within expectations...the index will likely turn positive this month" on year-end holiday spending, lower base in December 2008, says economist with local brokerage. Still, inflation "likely benign" in 2010, says economist, with no pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hike rates early. Statistics Department says November's decrease due to lower transportation costs (down 6.8% on year) but food and non-alchoholic beverages +0.9% on year. CPI rose 0.3% on-month in November. For January-November period, index +0.5% on year; economist says full year inflation likely less than official forecast of +1.0%. CPI grew 5.4% in 2008. (KPL)\par \par \uc2 0914 GMT [Dow Jones] Sterling slips to the day's lows ahead of the 0930 GMT BOE December minutes release. UK press speculation that more QE or a change in the deposit rate may have been discussed are factors weighing on the pound Wednesday. GBP/USD trades at 1.5940, just off Tuesday's 2-month low of 1.5924 while EUR/GBP is just a couple of ticks under Tuesday's one-week high of 0.8945. (GST)\par \par \uc2 0908 GMT [Dow Jones] USD is hardly changed with a little scattered profit-taking as trading dries up ahead of Christmas. With Japan closed, turnover has slumped but the general mood remains positive with European stocks opening higher. USD is expected to remain well supported with more positive economic news likely later Wednesday, following Tuesday's strong rise in existing home sales. USD is flat at Y91.81 while EUR is also hardly changed at $1.4251. GBP is down at $1.5949 as the market waits for the latest Bank of England minutes. (NEH)\par \par \uc2 0907 GMT [Dow Jones] French consumer spending in November was mostly supported by the government's car scrapping scheme, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 's economist Tullia Bucco. She says households made the most of the program, which will end in January. "Today's outcome lifts spending on manufactured goods more than 2% above the 3Q average, pointing to a sizeable contribution to 4Q private consumption." She sees GDP +0.5 to +0.6% in 4Q, after the disappointing performance recorded in 3Q. "However, looking ahead, this pace of GDP growth looks hardly sustainable in the coming quarters, when government stimulus measures will fade or will become decisively less supportive." (GAM) [ 12-23-09 0441ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091223e5cn00054\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
