\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Higher; Tipped In 9,550-9,650 Range\b0\par\par\uc2 1,435 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 November 2009\par 02:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0145 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR higher at 9,590 vs 9,560 late yesterday as risk aversion resurfaces and foreign players still spooked by talk of possible measures taken by Bank Indonesia to limit short-term fund inflows, dealers say. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  says if Bank Indonesia imposes restrictions on foreign holdings of SBI paper, measures would result in moderate, albeit temporary, demand for USD; "however, in our view, the underlying trend will remain IDR positive." Cautions against a sanguine view that Bank Indonesia won't act - for instance, it could prevent foreign investors from rolling existing positions in SBI and entering new ones. Dealers peg USD/IDR in 9,550-9,650 range, expecting Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  later today.(IMS)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0142 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds little moved in early trade, while futures trading with moderate losses as foreigners snap 14-session buying streak and reverse into net-sellers; 3-year yield +1 bp at 4.25%, 5-year yield down 1 bp at 4.77%, December futures down 5 ticks at 109.70, with foreigners selling 1,406 contracts in net so far today. "Other than foreign selling in futures, the overall favorable atmosphere for bonds hasn't changed much, while weaker domestic stocks will likely help sentiment," says \uc2 Daishin Securities\uc2  analyst Jeong Im-bo. Tips 3-year yield in 4.20%-4.30% range for day. Notes, longer end bonds may perform well, given their attractive yields. Whether foreigners increase net selling volume in futures or not could be key, though Jeong doesn't expect any aggressive selling. (MJL)\par \par \uc2 0141 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares last down 0.4% at 7732.64 on weak U.S., regional shares, led by electronics stocks (down 0.5%) on profit taking, weakness in U.S. techs. LCD maker \uc2 Chi Mei Optoelectronics\uc2  (3009.TW) down 1.0% at NT$20.60 after strong gains this week on \uc2 Innolux Display\uc2 's (3481.TW) acquisition. Selling only mild; "investors know the government will not let local shares fall much before the municipal government elections (early next month)," says Hua Nan Securities Investment Management trader Henry Miao. Expects main index to consolidate in 7700-7760 band rest of session in absence of major leads. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Perng yesterday says up to NT$300 billion worth of foreign money shouldn't remain on island, but "foreign investors may put the money in the bourse, which will help the market instead," says Miao. Formosa Plastics (1301.TW) down 0.6% at NT$65.80 as group's NT$280 billion integrated petrochemical complex expansion project held up because of difficulty in getting larger pollution emissions quota.(JEH)\par \par \uc2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY falling, with Japan exporters eyed selling a lot due to this regular settlement day, in addition to selling by U.S. names in Japan position adjustments ahead of U.S. holiday season, says senior sales dealer at major bank in Tokyo. Adds weak Japan stocks, which often negative for EUR, also fueling selling by short-term players; EUR/USD also down. EUR/JPY may fall to 132.00 vs last 132.60; EUR/USD may fall to 1.4850 vs last 1.4903. AUD/JPY may fall to 80.50 vs last 81.69.(TMO)\par \par \uc2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB steady at 33.19 from late yesterday with pair unmoved by weekly U.S. jobless claims, which offered no surprises; BOT suspected of again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in market, although only mildly, trader says. "The market should be fairly subdued after there was nothing unexpected overnight and also as we head into the weekend," says local dealer. Pair expected in 33.15-33.25 band.(LHM)\par \par \uc2 0132 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks retreat as investors lighten positions amid cautious tone following Wall Street's losses. STI off 0.2% at 2753.84; support tipped at 2727 (Nov. 13 close), with resistance at year-to-date high of 2785. Market breadth at almost two decliners for every gainer, while overall volume light. Dealer at local brokerage says near term downside could be limited, but whether market can attain new highs remains to be seen; "looking at the charts, you'll see that the lows are getting higher and higher while the highs have remained steady. It's now a question of whether the market can go up some more." Top percentage decliners among blue chips include NOL (N03.SG), off 1.3% at S$1.57, ST Engineering (S63.SG), off 1.0% at S$3.13. (FKH)\par \par \uc2 0132 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December Kospi200 futures down 0.2% at 213.25, but investors mostly sidelined after yesterday's massive foreign stock buying via program buying in non-arbitrage trade; that "seems to be just a one-off event. It's still not clear why they picked up such a heavy number of stocks yesterday," leaving investors in doubt about sustainability of foreign stock buying, says Moon Ju-hyun at \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2 . Foreigners' net selling in spot market so far improves market basis, spurring program buying in arbitrage trade now. But lead Kospi200 expected to drift around last close of 213.70 rest of today. (SKS)\par \par \uc2 0130 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares may retreat on Wall Street, technical cues; benchmark PSEi may slip to initial 3050 support after rising to close +1.0% at 3083.30 Thursday; index +2.4% over last 3 days; expected near-term trading band 3000-3100; "We'll likely see the market move sideways, with a downward bias because of the overdue correction. The market needs a technical breather," says Grace Cerdenia, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 2TradeAsia.com; blue chip Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH), gold producer \uc2 Philex Mining\uc2  (PX.PH), among market's recent gainers, may lead market retreat. (SFL)\par \par \uc2 0130 GMT [Dow Jones] 10-year cash JGB yield likely rangebound in 1.270%-1.370% band over next two weeks, as players unlikely to chase aggressively without further stock falls, JPY gains or increasing speculation over additional BOJ easing steps, says Deutsche Securities strategist Makoto Yamashita; 10-year yield last down 0.5 bp at 1.290%. Says market will closely watch BOJ's view on deflation at Gov. Shirakawa press conference from 0630 GMT, but thinks BOJ chief unlikely to clearly acknowledge Japan entering deflation as says BOJ wants to maintain economic recovery scenario. (MEF)\par \par \uc2 0125 GMT [Dow Jones] "There is no evidence central banks are diversifying away from USD," says \uc2 State Street Global Advisors\uc2  Head of \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Management Collin Crownover. Notes IMF data which shows \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves remain consistent; "there is no other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  right now that has the same credibility as USD," he says. Says EUR not liked as alternative \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to lack of transparency in institutions, depth of market, uncertainty about future of EU project. CNY also won't be a reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for some time. Says USD weakness fully consistent with cyclical explanation, driven by the relative economic performance and fiscal, monetary policy; "We don't see anything abnormal about USD weakness at this stage of the decline." (EGC)\par \par \uc2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] December Nikkei 225 futures down 1.0% at 9450, just off low of 9440, as weak tech stocks hurting cash index; next support around 200-day moving average (now 9341), but futures may fall more next week, with technical support for next week pegged at July 13 intraday low of 9030, says Traders Securities manager Tamotsu Numazaki. While financial stocks higher today on cash market on short-covering, equity financing concerns will linger, he says; adds, if yen remains strong and continues to weigh on exporter stocks, market sentiment unlikely to improve any time soon.(JUO)\par \par \uc2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered does not expect \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity squeeze from latest regulations on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity and \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions by South Korean government, as impact on local banks isn't likely to be large and no direct measures were introduced on foreign bank branches. But says, "a new guideline on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedging transactions confirms \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities' determination to slow the pace of KRW appreciation." Note, South Korea's Financial Services Commission says yesterday it will limit size of forward \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions that South Korean companies can enter into, to a total value of not more than 125% of the underlying transactions they are hedging against, which could mean less then expected selling of USD forwards by local exporters in future. (MJL) [ 11-19-09 2045ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091120e5bk0001f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
