\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Indonesia Shares End Down 0.6%; Bias To Stay Weak\b0\par\par\uc2 1,130 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2009\par 10:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0943 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares end lower on profit taking after gaining more than 4% in previous 6 sessions. Main index ends down 0.6% at 2468.788 in moderate volume, off intraday high of 2491.007. Traders say fresh weakness of IDR also inspire selling on worries it could increase overseas debt of many companies. "It's a healthy correction after recent gains," says trader at First Asia Capital; expects selling to continue tomorrow ahead of weekend, with index likely testing support of 2450. Among decliners were Car distributor Astra (ASII.JK), down 2.9% at IDR33,250, coal miner Bukit Asam (PTBA.JK), down 2.3% at IDR15,,000, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Mandiri\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BMRI.JK) was off 1.6% at IDR4,725. (EDH)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0941 GMT [Dow Jones] Sterling is mixed after October retail sales came in at +0.4% on the month against consensus of +0.5% and public finance deteriorated further with PSNB hitting a record high for October. GBP/USD is a couple of ticks higher at 1.6682 while EUR/GBP is 5 ticks higher at 0.8915. (GST)\par \par \uc2 0940 GMT [Dow Jones] The Swedish unemployment rate fell to 8.1% in October from 8.3% in September and was lower than expected, says Handelsbanken. "The decline in unemployment was due to weak labour supply and not strong employment, which dampens some of the initial joy," Handelsbanken says. Expects unemployment to increase in coming months before peaking in spring 2010. Sweden's two-year bond yields is down one basis point at 0.58% while the ten-year yield is three basis points higher at 3.28%. (ANM)\par \par \uc2 0939 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts fall as UK retail sales records the strongest annual rise for 17 months, up 3.4% versus 3.0% forecast. October PSNB rose more than expected to GBP11.4 billion versus GBP7 billion forecast and October PSNCR rose to GBP5.9 billion versus GBP5.5 billion forecast. The December gilt futures contract is currently down 0.05 on the day at 118.27, from 118.36 prior to the data. The 10-year benchmark gilt is down 0.07 at 106.365, yielding 3.682%, from 3.677% prior to the data. (NKA)\par \par \uc2 0934 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI ends +0.1% at 1276.65 with debutante Maxis (6012.KU) closing +8.4% at MYR5.42 on Main Market debut. Market breadth negative with decliners outpacing gainers 457 to 238 on persistent profit-taking by retail investors. "The Maxis listing created a short-term buzz in the market but this quickly died down with retail players happy to cash in on gains in excess of 13% (from retail price of MYR4.75)," says one dealer, tipping benchmark to trade in 1270-1285 range Friday. Maybank's analyst Lee Cheng Hooi says retail investors were selling into market strength; expects short term price volatility. Among gainers, AMMB (1015.KU) +1.0% at MYR5.12, \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2  (1961.KU) +0.7% at MYR5.54 and Genting (3182.KU) +0.7% at MYR7.13. On downside, Dialog (7277.KU) down 5.5% at MYR1.37, RHB Capital (1066.KU) down 1.5% at MYR5.39 and Jetson (9083.KU) down 6.3% at MYR2.70. (VGB)\par \par \uc2 0931 GMT [Dow Jones] The Polish zloty is lower Thursday morning in anticipation of weak industrial output figures for October, a trader says. Adds the zloty is seen in a relatively wide range of EUR/PLN 4.08-4.14. EUR/PLN now at 4.1267 from 4.1023 late Wednesday, USD/PLN at 2.7770 from 2.7436. Average yield of 2-year bonds much weaker at 5.11% from 4.90%, 5-year at 5.64% from 5.61%, and 10-year at 6.13% from 6.16%. (MMS)\par \par \uc2 0927 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR sharply higher at 9,560 in late session vs 9,410 late yesterday on resurfacing risk aversion which drives USD higher vs most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , combined with fears of capital control and year-end demand from local companies, dealers say. "The confluence of the three factors pushed the dollar higher sharply today, which prompted Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a dealer says; Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected of selling around $100 million to defend local unit today. Top central bankers also repeat assurance it has no plan to restrict capital inflows, but dealers says it will take some time to calm market. 9,500-9,600 range tipped Friday. (IMS)\par \par \uc2 0926 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF has just printed a near 3 week high and is testing trendline resistance at 1.5135. \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says a sustained break above here will open the topside toward the wider range highs between 1.5245-50. EUR/CHF now at 1.5135. (GST)\par \par \uc2 0918 GMT [Dow Jones] Broad based signs of divergence imply advanced profit taking in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . However, the killer trend-ending signals are so far absent so consolidation into next week, rather than a major change in USD sentiment is the name of the game says the bank. (GST)\par \par \uc2 0916 GMT [Dow Jones] A retreat in equities and a decline in the price of gold from recent record highs helped to push the USD higher in places. Comments by Fed's Plosser that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is just as concerned about deflation as it with inflation also appeared top encourage the move. The USD is flat at Y89.35 but the EUR has fallen sharply to $1.4879 and the GBP is down at $1.6686. (NEH)\par \par \uc2 ("MARKET TALK: UK Retail Sales, M4 May Give QE Hints - LBG," at 0910 GMT, misstated time of the data . The correct version follows:)\par \par \uc2 UK October retail sales and M4 numbers hit the wires at 0930 GMT. \uc2 Lloyds Banking Group\uc2  says a stronger than expected retail print and an improvement in money supply would add to the view that the BOE will not extend QE further in February. The DJN forecast for retail sales is +0.6% on the month and 3% on the year, PSNB is seen +7B, PSNCR +5.5B. (GST)\par \par \uc2 0910 GMT [Dow Jones] UK October retail sales and M4 numbers hit the wires at 0830 GMT. \uc2 Lloyds Banking Group\uc2  says a stronger than expected retail print and an improvement in money supply would add to the view that the BOE will not extend QE further in February. The DJN forecast for retail sales is +0.6% on the month and 3% on the year, PSNB is seen +7B, PSNCR +5.5B. (GST) [ 11-19-09 0443ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091119e5bj0006s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
