\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Bank Indonesia Downplays Talk Of Capital Controls\b0\par\par\uc2 By Farida Husna and I Made Sentana   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 388 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2009\par 06:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Bank Indonesia is "comfortable" with how it manages the rupiah's exchange rate and has no immediate plan to limit capital inflows, Deputy Governor Hartardi Sarwono said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Sarwono said that while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is evaluating the impact of limiting foreign ownership of short-term notes issued by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also known as SBIs, no changes are in the works.\par \par \uc2 "Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still comfortable enough with the existing policies, namely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (in the market) if there's a big fluctuation in the (rupiah) exchange rate," Sarwono told Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Some economies such as Taiwan and Brazil recently imposed measures to discourage short-term capital inflows to slow the appreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Indonesia is similarly witnessing a surge in hot money inflows following two peaceful elections earlier this year. It is also an investor favourite for being one of the few countries recording growth while others grapple with recession.\par \par \uc2 Sarwono added that purchases and sales of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's paper by foreign investors remain orderly and haven't caused any sharp fluctuation in the rupiah's exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 He said that foreign investors currently own IDR47 trillion ($4.98 billion) of the total IDR280 trillion of SBI in the market.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors also own around $11 billion of rupiah-denominated government bonds.\par \par \uc2 Despite of the rupiah rising 17% against the dollar so far this year, several Bank Indonesia officials recently made bullish comments about the local unit. Analysts say the remarks suggest the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  favors further appreciation to contain inflation, which is expected to accelerate next year.\par \par \uc2 Standard Chartered Bank economist Eric Sugandi said a stronger rupiah wouldn't severely hurt the Indonesian economy as it isn't heavily reliant on exports, unlike some of its regional peers.\par \par \uc2 A stronger rupiah will also reduce the cost of capital goods imported by domestic companies, which will give the economy an eventual boost.\par \par \uc2 He added that Bank Indonesia should be careful in imposing capital controls as such an act could discourage foreign investors from entering Indonesia, hurting the rupiah.\par \par \uc2 -By Farida Husna and I Made Sentana; Dow Jones Newswires; 62-21 39831277; I-Made.Sentana@dowjones.com [ 18-11-09 0526GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091118e5bi000bc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
