\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  Min: Not Aiming To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  In  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  At Any Particular Level\b0\par\par\uc2 162 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2009\par 07:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Indonesian Finance Minister Sri Mulyani Indrawati said Wednesday that the government isn't looking to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at any particular level, but will ensure that any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves are smooth.\par \par \uc2 Speaking on the sidelines of the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation summit here, she said Indonesia isn't seriously affected by the weaker dollar because the country is mainly an exporter of natural resources rather than of manufactured goods.\par \par \uc2 She said, however, that sudden moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market are undesirable that given the recent depreciation of the dollar.\par \par \uc2 She also said that Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must take future inflation expectations into account when setting monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 She cited uncertainty over rising commodity prices and a weaker dollar as possible drivers of inflation.\par \par \uc2 -By David Roman, Dow Jones Newswires; +65 64154 045; david.roman@dowjones.com [ 11-11-09 0623GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091111e5bb000l8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw442\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6630\pichgoal600
