\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Indonesia Bond Market Makes Late Move Off Lows\b0\par\par\uc2 1,286 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Oktober 2009\par 10:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0932 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia bond market regains some ground as IDR reverses slide; yields now largely steady vs Wednesday after rising 10-15 bps early. "There are genuine flows from offshore, they're buying in the belly of the curve," dealer says; "it looks like the first phase of some real money accounts coming back in and starting to bet against the dollar again." Dealers say FR30 7-year benchmark yield around 9.78% vs 9.7487% Wednesday; FR36 10-year at 10.31% vs 10.1890%. USD/IDR lower at 9,610 late vs 9,690 Wednesday; hit 9,710 earlier but dragged back by suspected heavy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Stocks also claw back most of their early losses to close down 0.5%.(RLC)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0930 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR forward premium falls more amid easing bond yields, overnight index swaps since RBI kept key policy rates unchanged at monetary policy review Tuesday offering comfort that interest rates would remain low at least in near-term; September 2010 premium at 1.25 vs 1.30 while October premium at 1.36 vs 1.42 last close, dealer with private bank tips continuing receiving interest as traders sell spot dollars to enter buy/sell swaps in forwards. Higher USD/INR spot since RBI policy also attracting supplies from exporters, dealer adds. "There was paying interest from some large foreign banks a few times but it could not withstand the continuing exporter supplies," says dealer. (AVK)\par \par \uc2 0930 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB unchanged in late trade at 33.46, with selling from offshore pulling pair back after early rise to 33.51 which came on broad USD strength offshore; pair overall in tight 33.47-33.51 range. "The \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2 's revision of its GDP forecast didn't really affect the market," says Bangkok-based dealer, because more positive growth is already expected in 2010. Players eyeing U.S. 3Q GDP later for guidance, but expects 33.35-33.50 range for USD/THB tomorrow. (LHM)\par \par \uc2 0927 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR turns south late after suspected heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and capital inflows; now 9,610 vs 9,690 late Wednesday, off intraday high 9,725. "BI came in heavily around 9,700 and after that the market turned around by itself," dealer says. Dealers estimate \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $150 million-$200 million. Bank Indonesia deputy governor Hartadi Sarwono says solid economic fundamentals give scope for rupiah to strengthen further, tips dollar to stay under pressure; dealer adds London-based investors sell pair likely to buy local bonds; 9,580-9,650 tipped Friday. (RLC, IMS)\par \par \uc2 0924 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI ends off low, down 0.6% at 1241.75 with decliners outpacing gainers 2 to 1 on concerns over pace of economic recovery after poor U.S. economic data, say dealers. "The decline was in tandem with losses in other Asian markets. Foreign funds were all net sellers in some of the big cap stocks but volume traded was moderate," says dealer. Cautions, benchmark KLCI closed below psychological 1250 level, indicating more weakness may be in pipeline. "The performance on Wall Street (tonight) will be closely watched. Further declines may drag the KLCI well below its immediate support at 1233 (30-DMA)," he adds. On upside, expects resistance at 1250. Among decliners, Genting (3182.KU) down 1.5% at MYR7.20, Maybank (1155.KU) down 1.8% at MYR6.63. On upside, Latexx (7064.KU) adds 1.9% at MYR2.65 and SP Setia (8664.KU) +1.3% MYR3.86. (VGB)\par \par \uc2 0917 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 France Telecom\uc2  (FTE) 5-year CDS is little changed Thursday, quoted at 54/57bps from Wednesday's close of 54bps, according to Markit. \uc2 France Telecom\uc2  posted in-line 3Q results. \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says the company's ability to grow organically is under pressure and the bank says its appetite for M&A may increase. "We believe that even relatively small transactions could be sufficient to damage its perception as a 'safe haven'". Keeps its marketweight recommendation on bonds, and is neutral on CDS. (SYC)\par \par \uc2 0913 GMT [Dow Jones] Greek government bond spreads are wider in mid-morning trade after credit ratings agency \uc2 Moody's\uc2  warns of a possible downgrade. "The market has reacted to the \uc2 Moody's\uc2  announcement," says a bond trader. "The spread on the 10 year GGB over German bunds has widened to 140 basis points compared with 135 basis points earlier in the week." (AKG)\par \par \uc2 0913 GMT [Dow Jones] This week's surge in the dollar should be a gift to dollar bulls like the analysts at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . "Yet we are skeptical," the bank says. Sure, this rush away from risk could deter some of the trend-following dollar bashers who have dominated the market of late. But current risk nerves could easily prove to be brief, the bank says. Fed hiking plans are needed for a real USD boost, bank adds. A strong US GDP reading later Thurs could help support that, bank says. But it could also pump up risk bets and send it lower. EUR/USD trades at 1.4751. (KJM)\par \par \uc2 0908 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP at 7-week high 47.73 late Thursday, lifted by broad USD strength from 47.475 late Wednesday; volume heavy as pair moved within 47.60-47.81 band; pair may still rise, possibly testing 48 if awaited U.S. economic data further saps market of risk appetite; support 47.50. "The market now awaits U.S. GDP due tonight. I still think the dollar could advance, but let's wait for the GDP numbers," says trader. (SFL)\par \par \uc2 0907 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD is mostly lower as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in general stabilize ahead of the 3Q USD GDP data due later in the day. Even if good, the figures are unlikely to halt the recent rise in risk aversion but investors appears keen to flatten positions just in case. Underlying sentiment remains poor after the RBNZ fails to hint at early rate hikes and US data continued to disappoint. Global stock markets are lower and the price of crude has fallen once again. The EUR, though, is up at $1.4745, while the USD has fallen to Y90.70. The GBP is also up at $1.6420. (NEH)\par \par \uc2 0857 GMT [Dow Jones] Albert Edwards at SG is nervous. "They say the trend is your friend until it hits a bend," he says. "Beware: we may just have hit one." The current pullback in risky assets could be nothing more than a glitch on the path to a global recovery, of which we have seen several in recent months. But Edwards points out that we're now seeing the first turn down in the weekly ECRI leading indicator since December last year. Remember that Japan's "lost decade" produced false dawns, and watch leading indicators closely, he warns. (KJM)\par \par \uc2 0855 GMT [Dow Jones] Italy is well positioned with the yearly funding, and the amount of treasury bonds, or BTPs, on auction is decreasing, thus "this should be a supportive factor at today's auction," says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  strategist Giuseppe Maraffino, ahead of Italy's up to EUR7.5 billion auction of BTP July 2012 and BTP March 2020 and July 2016-dated floating rate note. The auction comes in a favorable period for periphery, with the current spread of around 68 basis points between BTP and German bunds expected to tighten to 55-60 basis points in the next three months, he says. (BEM) [ 10-29-09 0532ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091029e5at0006d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
