\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Higher; Ctrl Bk Eyed Selling At 9600\b0\par\par\uc2 1,404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2009\par 02:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0139 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR higher at 9,600 vs 9,585 late yesterday on broad USD strength, dealers say; add Bank Indonesia likely sold around $20 million at 9,600 to slow pair's rise. "The amount of Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was small, which indicates that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is not aiming to stop the dollar's rise, but only to slow it down," dealer says. Pair tipped at 9,575-9,625 for day.(IMS)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0138 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares open up on technical rebound after yesterday's 2.8% tumble, but investors still harbor concerns that market will run out of catalysts amid signs that global economic recovery won't be smooth; "we're particularly worried about the U.S. economy because there may be ugly surprises as it rebounds," says Ping An Securities analyst Li Xianming. Shanghai Composite +0.2% at 3027.05, tipped to trade in 2950-3050 range today. Among actives, Citic Securities (600030.SH) +0.2% at CY27.23, Sinopec (600028.SH) +0.9% at CNY11.75. Shenzhen Index +0.4% at 1059.48. (NYW)\par \par \uc2 0134 GMT [Dow Jones] RBA likely to hike by 25 bps next week given today's CPI numbers but there's some risk of a bigger move, says UBS Economist Scott Haslem; "with upstream price pressures easing, lower concentration of price gains and no evident acceleration in today's quarterly print, we continue to look for a 25 bps hike from the RBA next week and an ongoing march to 4.25% by early March next year, though today's data does leave the risk of a 50 bps hike on the table were the RBA to see merit in finishing the year early, given next month's jobs and retail data is likely to be less buoyant."(EGC)\par \par \uc2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares called lower amid falls in some other Asian markets, lack of local cues; benchmark PSEi may drift towards 2900 support, says Oliver Plana, head of sales at Asiasec Equities, after falling 0.2% yesterday to 2935.74, with resistance at 2950. "We expect the market to move sideways. Regional markets are not giving (strong) leads"; blue chips like PLDT (TEL.PH), \uc2 Manila Electric\uc2  (MER.PH) may fall as investors shift funds to small caps.(SFL)\par \par \uc2 0132 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia 3Q CPI broadly in line with expectations with annualized inflation continuing to run above ceiling of RBA's target range, supporting another 25 bps hike next Tuesday, says Su-Lin Ong, senior economist at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 . CPI data highlight rising cost of living despite some respite from stronger AUD. Says core inflation continued to moderate in on-year terms, will likely move back into the RBA's 2%-3% target range in coming quarters but stronger economy, resilient labor market suggests core inflation likely to trough at higher level that thought. Coupled with the RBA's view of return to trend growth next year, rather sticky core inflation picture argues for return to neutral cash rate (5%-6%) in 2010 rather than just the removal of excessive stimulus. (JEG)\par \par \uc2 0130 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds higher on U.S. Treasurys, Taiex's 0.2% drop, also as banks park their excess funds in bond market, says local brokerage trader. But doesn't expect further gains amid consolidating global bond markets; adds current yields may be "too low for brokerage firms to hold", so trade mostly driven by banks' demand, some speculation. 10-year yield last at 1.3915% vs 1.3950% last close, eyed in 1.3700%-1.4000% band for day. 5-year yield at 0.9000% vs 0.9135% last close, tipped in 0.8700%-0.9100% band.(WUM)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY hits 1-week low of 135.27 on selling by Japan exporters for month-end settlement, with knock-on effects for USD/JPY (last at 91.45), says dealer at major Tokyo bank; adds USD/JPY, EUR/JPY "likely to keep facing downward pressure into the weekend, because there are still massive sell orders placed by Japanese exporters." Tips EUR/JPY support at 135.00 vs last 135.40, USD/JPY support at 91.00. Meanwhile, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 's Koji Fukaya says EUR/JPY may decline to 134.00 in coming days.(MIN)\par \par \uc2 0123 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB slightly higher at 33.44-33.46 vs 33.43 late yesterday, tracking general USD gains vs Asians; trading thin. Risk aversion likely to inspire players to unwind their short positions, says local dealer. Adds foreign investors being net sellers for 4 straight sessions of THB3.1 billion in local stock market may drive baht to weakening side. Resistance at 33.50, support put at 33.43. Foreign investors have been net buyers of THB60.46 billion year to date. They were net buyers of THB23 billion in September vs THB5.23 billion month to date. (PIY)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 0.5% at 4731.7, hits 2-week low of 4730.9, tentatively breaking key support from 30 day moving average and minor channel support line at 4740.0. Index could soon test 50-day moving average and lower Bollinger band support near 4650, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. Fall coincides with above-consensus 1.0% rise in 3Q CPI. Banks leading the market down, with NAB (NAB.AU) down 1.6% on profit taking despite better-than-expected full year cash profit. Materials also down on bearish offshore leads, with BHP (BHP.AU) down 0.7%, Bluescope (BSL.AU) down 2.8%, Boral (BLD.AU) down 4.8%, \uc2 Incitec Pivot\uc2  (IPL.AU) down 3.6%. Defensive sectors outperforming, with \uc2 Telstra\uc2  (TLS.AU) up 1.5%, Woolworths (WOW.AU) up 1.0%, CSL (CSL.AU) up 1.0%, Foster's (FGL.AU) up 0.9%, \uc2 AGL Energy\uc2  (AGK.AU) up 0.2%. "I think it cracks," says Southern Cross Equities director Charlie Aitken, referring to the support. "Banks are tired. BHP is tired. That's 50% of the index." (DWR)\par \par \uc2 0120 GMT [Dow Jones] HK September trade improved, led by China demand, but genuine recovery yet to come, says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . Official data yesterday show total exports down 8.6% on year in September vs August's 13.9% drop, with improvement almost entirely attributable to shipments to China. House expects merchandise trade flows will see significant narrowing in on-year declines in 4Q but primarily driven by low base effect, while trade activity as a whole has yet to recover to pre-crisis levels. "Although we believe that the worst contractions for the trade sector are behind us, it will take time for demand from developed markets to recuperate after the worst shock in decades."(YKS)\par \par \uc2 0116 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD flat at 7.7501 but biased up as expected fall in local stocks could slow inflows, says senior trader at local bank. "I've spotted some signs of a correction in local stocks after the Hong Kong government unveiled measures in an attempt to cool the property market. Also, U.S. stocks languished overnight and this could weigh on the local equities." Notes HKMA sold HK$1.55 billion in NY trade overnight to defend \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg. Tips pair in 7.7500-7.7505 band near term.(YKS)\par \par \uc2 0116 GMT [Dow Jones] Economists generally agree with BOK forecast that Korea current account surplus will easily exceed $30 billion this year, with Daishin Economic Research Institute economist Kim Yoon-gi saying even $40 billion looks possible; "a faster than expected recovery in exports and a sharper decline in imports (than for exports) finally resulted in a much larger than expected surplus, leading both the BOK and economists to revise up their forecasts" for year." Big current account surplus means abundant USD supply and "is definitely forming a trend of falling dollar/won over the mid- to long-term, which may push it to between 1,100 and 1,150 by the year end" vs 1,194 current level for pair. For now, corrective mood in stock markets is limiting data impact. Current account surplus more than doubled to $4.20 billion in September.(KKA) [ 10-27-09 2139ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091028e5as0001n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
