\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,163 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2009\par 08:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =2ND UPDATE: CHINA DATA RAISE LIKELIHOOD OF POLICY TWEAKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The continued pickup in China's economic growth in the third quarter raises the likelihood that Beijing will begin to tweak its stimulus policies next year, as it turns its attention toward the risks of excess capacity and inflation. (published 0435 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN REFORM MINISTER SENGOKU: FISCAL 2009 TAX REVENUE COULD BE SHARPLY BELOW Y40T \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese administrative reform minister Yoshito Sengoku said Thursday that the government's tax revenue could fall well below Y40 trillion in the current fiscal year ending March, adding to worries that Tokyo may end up selling a record Y50 trillion or more of new debt in the year. (published 0230 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: JAPAN TRADE SURPLUS HIGHEST IN 18 MONTHS, BUT GROWTH LIKELY TO SLOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's merchandise trade surplus expanded to its largest level in 18 months in September, with the decline in exports slowing as overseas markets picked up somewhat, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Thursday. (published 0252 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA TSY SECY: ECONOMY SHOWING ENCOURAGING SIGNS OF RECOVERY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Treasury Secretary Ken Henry said Thursday that the domestic economy is showing "encouraging signs" of recovery. (published 0351 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA TRADE MIN: AUSTRALIAN DOLLAR RISE COMMODITY-DRIVEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A strengthening Australian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is being driven by rising commodity prices, Trade Minister Simon Crean said Thursday. (published 0138 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: VIETNAM \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : NOT DEVALUING DONG -REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Vietnam isn't devaluing the dong but flexibly adjusting its dollar exchange rate after the local unit rose to record this week, threatening to hurt growth at a time when exports and remittances have slumped. (published 0429 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: BANK OF KOREA TO STOP BUYING BANK DEBENTURES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea said Thursday it will end a one-year program to buy bank debentures and other special-purpose bonds introduced last November to help supply liquidity into the financial system. (published 0337 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA:NOT MULLING MEASURES ON FOREIGN BANKS' \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  LIQUIDITY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Korean government isn't considering imposing any new measures on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  liquidity of foreign banks operating in the country, an official from the Ministry of Strategy and Finance said Thursday, refuting recent media reports. (published 0309 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN FSA TO EXTEND SMALLER FIRMS NEW CREDIT LINE UP TO 3 YEARS-NIKKEI \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Financial Services Agency said it will establish a fund to provide fresh lines of credit to cash-strapped small and midsize companies, whose debts are not covered by public loans and credit facilities, the Nikkei reported Thursday. (published 0327 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par HKMA SELLS HK$5.04 BLN TO DEFEND US DLR PEG THURSDAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Thursday it sold HK$5.04 billion in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to defend the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar. (published 0313 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BANK INDONESIA SUSPECTED SOLD $50M TO SUPPORT RUPIAH -DEALERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was suspected to have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to arrest a slide in the rupiah Thursday, selling at least $50 million to prevent the dollar from breaking a key resistance of IDR9,500, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealers said. (published 0544 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DOWNER EDI\f28 \uc2 \f2  RAISES A$150M FROM MEDIUM TERM NOTE ISSUE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian engineering firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 Downer EDI Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said Thursday it raised A$150 million from the issue of medium-term notes priced at 375 basis points over swap after the offer closed oversubscribed. (published 0059 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: AUSTRALIA FUTURE FUND: GLOBAL BANK SECTOR STILL FACES RISKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Future Fund Chairman David Murray said Thursday the financial system has reached a point of "reasonable" stability but that the global banking sector still faces risks. (published 0436 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA WANTS TO BOOST TRADE IN SERVICES WITH JAPAN - MINISTER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia wants to use negotiations over a free trade agreement with Japan to boost trade in services, Trade Minister Simon Crean said Thursday ahead of a visit to Japan on Sunday through Tuesday. (published 0422 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDONESIA BEHIND SCHEDULE ON INFRASTRUCTURE STIMULUS - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's government has spent only about a quarter of the fiscal stimulus allocated for infrastructure development this year, the Jakarta Globe reported Thursday, citing official data. (published 0404 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDONESIA PLN HIRES BARCLAYS, \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  TO MANAGE DOLLAR BOND SALE-SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesian state-owned utility \f28 \uc2 \f2 PT Perusahaan Listrik Negara\f28 \uc2 \f2  has hired \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  and UBS AG to manage its planned fixed-rate U.S. dollar-denominated global bond sale, a person familiar with the deal said Thursday. (published 0229 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAICOM SETS COUPON ON 3-YEAR BONDS AT 5.25%, 5-YEAR BONDS AT 6.15% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thaicom PCL\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Thursday it would pay a coupon of 5.25% on the three-year bonds it plans to issue and 6.15% on a five-year tranche. (published 0340 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: FED'S ROSENGREN: PRESERVING BANK CAPITAL IN TIMES OF CRISIS KEY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank of Boston President Eric Rosengren opened a conference by his bank Wednesday on regulatory reform by offering support for ways banks can preserve capital better during times of crisis. (published 2131 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =WSJ: FED WEIGHS FORCING BANKS TO USE NEW KIND OF DEBT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Momentum is building within the Federal Reserve to force banks, especially large ones, to stock up on a new kind of debt that would convert into equity if they hit rocky times. (published 2158 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par      ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par WSJ(10/22) HEARD ON THE STREET: THE DOLLAR'S TROUBLEMAKING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar's move beyond $1.50 to the euro Wednesday has reignited concerns that the world's reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is done for. Such changes, however, don't happen overnight: It took a century and two world wars to knock the pound off its perch. (published 0118 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OPTIONS: YEN OPTIONS DOWN; COULD RISE ANYTIME IF DOLLAR SURGES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dollar/yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  options fell in Asia Thursday as demand for hedges decreased due to narrowly-traded spot rates, and dealers said the volatilities will keep declining, though the chance of a sudden surge remains. (published 0358 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =INTERVIEW:ASIA CORPORATE LOANS MAY TAKE 2 YRS TO RECOVER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the region continues to recover from the financial crisis, bank lending to Asian companies, other than those in China and Japan, won't grow at "meaningful" levels for around two years, although local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -loan markets have been developing as a source of funding, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s most senior investment banker in Asia. (published 0324 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =PREVIEW: SINGAPORE SEPTEMBER CPI LIKELY DOWN 0.1% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's consumer prices likely fell for a sixth consecutive month in September mainly due to the high base of a year earlier. (published 0501 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: LOOKING FOR THE RISK MANAGEMENT RENAISSANCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Regulation shouldn't crush innovation, and innovation's what's needed when it comes to improving how financial firms deal with risk. (published 2131 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =IN THE MONEY: BANKS HAVE NO MARGIN FOR ERROR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There is little margin for error in the banking business these days. (published 2144 GMT) [ 22-10-09 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091022e5am000en\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoa