\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECT: Indonesia Finance Min: Rupiah Needs To Be More Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 324 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2009\par 09:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ("=UPDATE: Indonesia Finance Min: Rupiah Needs To Be More Stable," published at 0558 GMT, misstated the name of the Indonesian finance minister in the second paragraph. The error was also contained in the article "Indonesia Fin Min: Rupiah Needs To Be More Stable," published at 0311 GMT. The correct version follows:)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Linda Silaen \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Indonesia's finance minister said Wednesday that the rupiah and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  appear to be seeking a "new equilibrium," and said that the government will monitor the local unit closely for signs of volatility to ensure that it trades in a more stable manner against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah and its counterparts are "in the process of finding a new equilibrium in line with their (underlying) fundamentals" following the global downturn, Sri Mulyani Indrawati told reporters. "The rupiah exchange rate has not yet reached an equilibrium point where it is comfortable."\par \par \uc2 Her comments echo views among many investors and analysts that the rupiah is retracing back to levels prior to the global economic downturn. The relatively resilient performance of the domestic economy and an improving political climate have boosted investor sentiment in Southeast Asia's largest economy.\par \par \uc2 The finance ministry and Bank Indonesia have said that a stronger rupiah helps fight imported inflation, but have warned against excessively sharp movements in the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  often \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to cap sharp moves but doesn't target a specific \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  range and lets the market move relatively freely. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar several times in recent weeks after it weakened sharply.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah has risen around 17% since January. The dollar recently traded around IDR9,400, unchanged from its close Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 -By Linda Silaen, Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 3983 1277; Reuben.Carder@dowjones.com [ 21-10-09 0727GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091021e5al000ol\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
