\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Indonesia Trade Min: Econ Can Growth 4.0%-4.5% This Yr\b0\par\par\uc2 465 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juli 2009\par 13:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 20 Jul 2009 07:15 EDT *DJ  Indonesia Trade Min: This Yr Inflation Expected 5%-6%\par \par \uc2 20 Jul 2009 07:16 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Min:Central Bk Will \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  To Curb Any Volatility\par \par \uc2 20 Jul 2009 07:16 EDT *DJ  Indonesia Trade Min: GDP Expected To Grow 5%-6% In 2010\par \par \uc2 20 Jul 2009 07:17 EDT *DJ  Indonesia Trade Min: 2010 Inflation Likely 4.5%-5.5%\par \par \uc2 20 Jul 2009 07:33 EDT =DJ  INTERVIEW: Indonesia Trade Min: Econ Can Grow 4.0%-4.5% This Yr\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By P.R. Venkat \par    OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Indonesia's trade minister said Monday that the economy is likely to grow 4.0% to 4.5% this year and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stands ready to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to curb volatility.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "What is important is to maintain a stable \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and curb volatility ... What we look for is stability in the exchange rate that reflects the underlying macroeconomic fundamentals," Trade Minister Mari Pangestu told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 She also said the recent suicide bombings in Jakarta would have a limited impact on markets, including the stock and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, and that economic fundamentals remain strong.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As well, economic growth in the first half was encouraging, despite the contraction in exports and imports and low growth in investments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Domestic consumption accounts for 70% of our growth and it was growing at 5.8% and the growth remains strong," Pangestu said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's economy expanded at 4.1% in the first six months of the year, with the inflation rate at 3.65%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Inflation is already coming down and we do believe that we can maintain inflation between 5% to 6% this year, and then for the next year, 4.5% to 5.5%," Pangestu said, adding that the economy is expected to grow 5% to 6% in 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On trade, the minister said exports are likely to contract at a much slower pace than her ministry initially estimated.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In the beginning of the year, we were projecting a 20% to 30% contraction. Now we have revised it to around a 15% contraction," Pangestu said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 She said the recent suicide bombings won't have much impact on portfolio investments in the country, as most investors look at the fundamentals of the economy, which remain strong.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Of course, we can expect travel and tourism to be affected in the short term (but) Indonesia has demonstrated its resilience ... and we will also be resilient in overcoming this current unfortunate situation."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By P.R. Venkat, Dow Jones Newswires; +65 64154 152; venkat.pr@-dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 20, 2009 07:33 ET (11:33 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020090720e57k0026w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
