\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia steady but rupiah, won hurt by liquidity fear\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Yao   \par \uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2009\par 07:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Feb 5 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah and South Korean won fell on Thursday due to renewed concerns over a dollar liquidity crunch, while other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  held steady ahead of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate decision due later in the day.\par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> fell as far as 11,950 per dollar, down almost 1.9 percent, as investors rushed to buy dollars amid fears of a shortage of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  "There is not enough dollars in the market," said a Jakarta-based trader, adding that Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the rupiah but the amount of dollars it sold in the market was small.\par \par \uc2  Broad dollar strength as well as rising short-term dollar funding costs were adding pressure on the rupiah, traders said.\par \par \uc2  The rupiah has fallen just over 7 percent against the dollar so far this year, the second worst performer in Asia after the South Korean won <KRW=>, which is down almost 9 percent.\par \par \uc2  Asian central banks are generally leaning towards tolerating further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness as exports collapsed amid the global downturn, but they still \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility.\par \par \uc2  The won <KRW=> fell as far as 1,385.9 per dollar, down half a percent from Wednesday's close, after a senior government official was quoted as saying the country may suffer more foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity shortage. [ID:nSEO42854]\par \par \uc2  Much of Asia was affected by a dollar crunch last year caused by the global financial crisis, with South Korea suffering the most as foreign investors pulled out from the country, contributing to the won's 25 percent slide in 2008.\par \par \uc2  The cost of interbank dollar funding in Asia had been steadily falling but it has picked up again this year, albeit moderately, amid concerns about mounting financial industry losses. [ID:nSP189798]\par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Philippine peso <PHP=> was shackled between 47.39 and 47.65 per dollar as investors looked to the dollar's direction ahead of the ECB's rate decision.\par \par \uc2  The ECB is widely expected to keep interest rates at 2.0 percent later on Thursday. [ECB/INT]\par \par \uc2  "There is no real flow; it's a rangebound market. The peso is basically following what's happening outside," said a trader in Manila.\par \par \uc2  The peso reacted little to official data on Thursday that showed January inflation hit a 10-month low at 7.1 percent.\par \par \uc2  The euro held steady versus the dollar <EUR=> on Thursday after falling sharply the previous day due to the downgrade of Russia's sovereign rating.  \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at 0535 GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        89.39          89.46       +0.08\par  Sing dlr        1.5082         1.5094       +0.08\par  Taiwan dlr      33.653         33.670       +0.05\par  Korean won     1381.50        1378.50       -0.22\par  Baht             34.92          34.94       +0.06\par  Peso             47.39          47.52       +0.27\par  Rupiah        11850.00       11725.00       -1.05\par  Rupee            48.82          48.81       -0.02\par  Ringgit         3.6185         3.6240   