\par\par \b \uc2 Bank Indonesia: Monitoring Rupiah, To Ensure Its Stability\b0\par\par\uc2 212 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2008\par 06:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Bank Indonesia is constantly monitoring the rupiah and will work to ensure it remains stable, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's governor said Friday.\par \par \uc2 "We will continue to monitor the rise and fall of the rupiah in the market, and will work to ensure the rupiah maintains a stable position at a level that is beneficial to our economy," Bank Indonesia Governor Boediono told reporters. "We are always in the market," he added.\par \par \uc2 Boediono didn't elaborate on what measures the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might take to ensure that the rupiah remains stable.\par \par \uc2 Traders and analysts note that Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has scaled back its direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, after spending around 6% of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in October in an attempt to force the dollar lower against the rupiah.\par \par \uc2 The dollar traded at IDR11,250 around 0600 GMT, slightly higher than its close at IDR11,050 Thursday, as investors sold the rupiah and local assets to buy dollars due to concerns that the failed U.S. bailout talks for automakers could further hurt Asian shares, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer said.\par \par \uc2 -By Farida Husna, contributing to Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 3983 1277; Reuben.Carder@dowjones.com [ 12-12-08 0608GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081212e4cc0005t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
